WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.119
OK, we'll get started.

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:11.490
This is the second of the
post-training lectures,

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.990
and we're going to talk about--

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:16.500
well, we're going to finish off
the lecture from Tuesday first,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.465
whatever remaining
content we have.

00:00:18.465 --> 00:00:20.090
And then we'll talk
about reinforcement

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.700
learning from
verifiable rewards, kind

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.900
of the new exciting thing
that's been happening over maybe

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.930
the last six months
or so and really

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.180
powering all of these
reasoning models

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.920
that you see dominating
the landscape.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.760
So before I get into
the verifiable rewards

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.260
part, the exciting math RL
content, I'm going to go back

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.640
and I'm going to
finish off RLHF.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.500
So this is a very, very quick
recap of the last few slides

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.710
from Tuesday's lecture.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.980
So we want to do
reinforcement learning

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.370
from human feedback, which is
the setting where we observe

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.470
pairwise preference data, which
of the two responses are better,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.380
and we'd like to get a language
model policy that maximizes some

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.110
of underlying reward for
this preference data.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.400
And if you remember,
DPO is this algorithm

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.210
that allows us to optimize
this reinforcement learning

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:13.940
objective.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.700
And this objective is hard
because our language model,

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.140
the policy, so to speak, is on
the bottom of this expectation.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.980
You're sampling from
your language model.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.700
You're not just
maximizing the likelihood

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.530
under the language model.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.743
And the way we're
going to do this,

00:01:28.743 --> 00:01:30.160
if you remember
the derivation, is

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.560
that we're going to make
the nonparametric assumption

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.360
that our policy class is
the set of all functions.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:36.820
We're going to
rewrite the reward

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:39.370
as a ratio of the
policies and then

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.957
plug that in to the
Bradley-Terry objective.

00:01:41.957 --> 00:01:43.540
And so what we're
going to do is we're

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:48.400
going to find a policy such that
the implied reward maximizes

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.430
the probability of
seeing the references--

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.870
or sorry, the pairwise
preferences that we see.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.360
So now this is great because
it's basically supervised

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.480
learning on a kind of
alternatively parametrized

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.750
objective, which is nice.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.520
DPO updates have
this following form.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.580
The reason why DPO has took
the world by storm for a bit--

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.380
a year ago or so--

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.085
is that it can be written in
this very nice form, where

00:02:16.085 --> 00:02:17.460
what you're going
to do is you're

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.230
going to take these
gradient steps which

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.670
are going to be
multiplied by beta--

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.155
that's your regularization.

00:02:23.155 --> 00:02:25.530
And you're going to have a
higher weight when your reward

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.197
estimates are wrong,
so you're to update

00:02:27.197 --> 00:02:31.710
more when you're implied
rewards are not correct.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.420
And then you're
going to increase

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.275
the likelihood of
the good example,

00:02:35.275 --> 00:02:37.900
and you're going to decrease the
likelihood of the bad example.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.910
As I was saying, if you remember
what I was saying last lecture,

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.520
really RL algorithms
kind of boil

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.200
down often to just
upweight the good stuff,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.430
downweight the bad stuff.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:53.460
And the subtlety really is in
deciding what the good stuff is

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.700
and how much to upweight.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.290
And so this is one particular
choice of doing that.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.620
And we'll see other
things happen.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.800
And so DPO works pretty
well for a while.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.760
Essentially, all the
open model releases

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.290
use some variant of a DPO
to do their post-training.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:16.730
And really it's just very easy
to get working compared to PPO.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:21.640
And so for a while, it was
really the dominant approach.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.890
In the time since
then, there was

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:27.265
a huge flood of papers
that was like asterisk PO.

00:03:27.265 --> 00:03:29.640
Basically, everyone wanted to
come up with a DPO variant,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.080
so there were dozens
and dozens of papers.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:33.580
There are so many
that I don't think

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:37.900
it's worth it to go over the
big ocean of such papers.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.578
I'll mention two, not
because necessarily I

00:03:40.578 --> 00:03:42.370
think they're the right
ones, but because I

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.130
think they're ones that
have been used recently

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:46.840
by people that
are really pushing

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.700
the limits of this kind of
DPO style post-training.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.270
One is SimPO and
SimPO just makes

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.380
a very simple modification,
or two simple modifications.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.710
The first one is to really
normalize the update size

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.110
by the length of the responses.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.673
We'll see this theme
appearing later.

00:04:03.673 --> 00:04:05.340
And the other thing
they do is they just

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.610
get rid of the reference.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.720
So now we've lost the
DPO mathematical argument

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.420
that what we're doing is
looking at ratios of policies,

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.860
but well, this is
more purely looks

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.482
something where we
upweight the good stuff

00:04:20.482 --> 00:04:21.690
and downweight the bad stuff.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:26.310
Under that underlying
motivation, SimPO, totally fine.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.250
There's other variants where
you don't do necessarily

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.380
this reference policy
removal where you just

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.280
normalize by length called
length-normalized DPO

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.960
that people have done.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.250
And these two forms--

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.480
DPO with length
normalization and SimPO,

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.930
were the things that were tried
pretty extensively by AI2 folks

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.270
when they did Tulu 3.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.902
And I think one thing
that I'll point out--

00:04:50.902 --> 00:04:52.610
I'm going to pause
for a moment because I

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.200
feel like this is an important
conceptual-- not conceptual,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.990
just an important
empirical point, which

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:59.330
is that in RL, a
lot of the findings

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.240
are very, very contingent
on the specific setting.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.500
So depending on the environment
in which you run it,

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.300
depending on the base
model that you have,

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.960
depending on the post-training
preferences that you're

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.170
running on, you will find
pretty different conclusions.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.250
And so one example of this--
so a bunch of AI2 folks

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:17.960
have been doing a
lot of really good

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.060
post-training empirical work.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.130
And they had one work where
they were comparing DPO and PPO,

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.300
and they had found that
PPO was better than DPO

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.070
because of maybe its on policy.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:32.510
And they showed this jump
exactly as the DPO to PPO gap.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.590
And then in a later
work in Tulu 3,

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:38.420
they found actually if you do
your SFT in a nicer better way,

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.940
actually, that just eats up
all of the gains of both PPO

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:41.910
and DPO.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.282
So neither of these
really get any gains.

00:05:44.282 --> 00:05:45.740
And the only thing
that does better

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.470
is maybe DPO with
normalization-- pretty different

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:49.230
conclusions.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.605
Of course, a lot is different
between this left and right

00:05:51.605 --> 00:05:54.200
paper, but it's not that one is
wrong and the other is wrong.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.620
It's really that you should
be careful about reading

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.320
too many generalized
conclusions about any

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.823
of this stuff on the
basis of one paper.

00:06:01.823 --> 00:06:02.990
So that's an important note.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.652
Even as I go and talk about PPO
and I go and talk about GRPO

00:06:06.652 --> 00:06:08.110
later in this, you
shouldn't really

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:12.690
take any single experimental
result necessarily as gospel.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:16.810
So the last thing I want
to end with for RLHF

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.800
is two important things.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.990
And one of them will really
just motivate, I think,

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.250
the entirety of today's lecture.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:23.417
So I think that's important.

00:06:23.417 --> 00:06:25.900
So the first thing
is overoptimization.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:29.930
So in some sense, this is just
overfitting in fancier naming.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.310
But it is a term that I think
is very important, because what

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.700
it's saying is essentially, as
you optimize your policy more

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.290
and more-- so think
about this x-axis

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:42.160
as how much RL I did--
initially, your reward goes up

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:42.890
and up and up.

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.730
But eventually,
the reward models

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.260
that you fitted on
human preferences

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.530
are diverging from
real human preferences.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.830
And the more you optimize,
you eventually diverge out.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:54.130
You're not doing any better.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:58.360
You're just optimizing but not
actually improving your rewards.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.770
So this overoptimization thing
appears basically everywhere

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:02.710
in RLHF.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:04.530
It is a very big problem.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:08.550
And so that is
kind of a concern.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:10.950
And overoptimization
is a phenomenon

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:13.050
that really happens
in many ways because

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:14.820
of the noisiness of
human preferences

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.690
and the complexities
of human preferences.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.340
So some of my students had
a study where they basically

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.390
did RLHF on human preferences.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.810
They did RLHF on noisy
versions of AI feedback

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.260
and then they did RLHF
on non-noisy versions

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:29.050
of human feedback.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:31.020
And you see clear
overoptimization

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.990
phenomena, both for humans
and for noisy AI feedback,

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:39.760
but not really for clean,
noiseless AI feedback.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.480
And so really, if you're
going to be post-training,

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:43.980
you should expect
to see curves that

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.188
look like things on the left.

00:07:45.188 --> 00:07:47.500
As you train your model
to do better and better

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.910
on your proxy rewards
that you've measured,

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:52.510
you're not necessarily going
to get better and better models

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:56.050
in terms of human
preference win rates.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:59.570
And then the other thing, which
is an important side note,

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.700
is that when you do RL--

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.968
remember that I said we're no
longer in probabilistic world.

00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:06.760
So when you're doing
supervised fine-tuning

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.300
or when you're
doing pretraining,

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:09.700
it's quite clear that
what you're doing

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:11.500
is you're trying to do
probabilistic modeling

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:12.375
on some distribution.

00:08:12.375 --> 00:08:14.510
You're doing distribution
matching for something.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:18.260
But RLHF, it's
all just a policy.

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.480
There's no underlying
necessarily distribution.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.380
And how this manifests
is that often you'll

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.280
get much less calibrated models.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.150
A number of results across
many different papers

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:32.440
have shown that RLHF
models, often, especially

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:35.690
at temperature 1, show much
more overconfident behavior.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.820
So this one was from one
of the Anthropic papers.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:40.720
This one was from, I
think, the GPT 4 release.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.390
This one was from a paper
that one of my postdocs did.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:46.500
In all of these cases,
essentially, the RLHF models

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.130
are much less calibrated.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:49.850
And maybe that's fine
because calibration

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:51.850
isn't part of the reward
that you're putting in,

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.770
so that's as designed.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.100
But you should be very careful
thinking of these models

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.680
as calibrated
probabilistic models, which

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.960
you might be tempted
to do if you're

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.710
coming into this from a
generative modeling background.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.600
OK.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.740
So up until now, we've
been thinking about RLHF

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:11.740
and all of these things.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:12.700
Actually, I'll
stop for a moment.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.680
If anyone has any final
questions for RLHF,

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:16.555
I'll answer them before
I go on because we're

00:09:16.555 --> 00:09:19.380
going to totally switch topics
to RL from verified rewards

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.630
in a moment.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:22.745
Yes?

00:09:22.745 --> 00:09:25.580
If we went to the human
preferences graph,

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:27.930
what are the two axes
that you're looking at?

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:28.480
This one?

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:29.130
Yeah.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.270
So the x-axis is proxy reward.

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:34.180
So here we're fitting
a reward model.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:35.357
We're not doing DPO here.

00:09:35.357 --> 00:09:37.440
So let's say we're doing
something like PPO, where

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.710
we fit a reward, a classifier,
and the x-axis is saying,

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:46.760
how well did my RL algorithm
optimize that reward classifier?

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.980
So this is success at
RL is on the x-axis.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.400
Y-axis is this is
like true win rates,

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.790
either measured by AI feedback,
here, or human feedback.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:00.620
So this is like real human
votes on the y-axis here.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.950
And so this is saying you might
think that succeeding at RL

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:05.930
will make you succeed
at the actual task

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.070
that you're trying
to optimize for.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:08.970
That's not the case.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:12.380
It will overfit at some point
for at least human preferences.

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:13.260
Yes.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.730
So the question-- if the
objective is the [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:22.310
Why is it really
that [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:25.130
is not like-- there are
log A minus log B minus XC

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.430
plus log of 4 just like
normal AC overview?

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:31.610
Are you just asking why don't
they pull the fractions out

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.810
in the log or?

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.150
Because I thought this
was a numeric computation

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:37.692
thing that when you take these
two ratios of taking the log

00:10:37.692 --> 00:10:39.900
it's easier to compute and
that's why we want to do--

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:41.000
No, no.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.180
I think taking ratios is
actually bad numerically.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.400
I think this is really
just like a save the space.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:50.290
The ratios are more compact, and
they're also intuitive objects.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.690
The moment you go to SimPo,
it becomes a different form

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.420
because you no longer have
the same data constant.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:55.003
Is that right?

00:10:55.003 --> 00:10:55.790
Yes, that's right.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.440
It's simply just like a very
different motivation for why

00:10:58.440 --> 00:10:59.440
you would do this thing.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:00.320
There's no ratios.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:03.550
That's a very fundamentally
different object.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:04.510
Yes.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:07.900
Onto the graph where you showed
the reward sort of hacking thing

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:13.540
on them, I was wondering how
the authors measure the ground

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:16.120
truth of the reward score?

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:20.440
Presumably, the reward
function is [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.220
Right.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:23.530
It's kind of a train test gap.

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:26.000
The x-axis here, you
have a train set,

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.510
you fit a reward model on
it, and you're measuring

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:29.930
that fitted reward model.

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:33.770
And the y-axis is fresh samples
from the true reward oracle.

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:36.860
And so they're measuring in
expectation the same thing,

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:40.100
but they're not measuring in
finite sample the same thing.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.620
This is the same train-test
gaps in machine learning.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:43.860
Same conceptual object.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.550
They are in expectation
measuring the same thing,

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:49.400
for any finite sample,
that's not the case.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:50.750
OK, good.

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:54.638
So I think all these questions
about reinforcement learning

00:11:54.638 --> 00:11:56.180
from human feedback
I think brings up

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:58.730
a good point, which is
that human feedback is

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.300
very difficult to optimize.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.270
It's very difficult to scale.

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:05.720
And so you might wonder, are
there other kinds more effective

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:07.890
RL that we can bring to
bear on post-training?

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:10.320
And that's going to get us here.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.180
So up until now, we've been
talking about ChatGPT 3.5

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:15.630
in that era of models.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:20.360
And now I think we want to
talk about o1 and the new set

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:22.260
of reasoning style models.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.230
And the way we're
going to get there

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:27.080
is this thinking
of saying, OK, we

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:28.533
have this very
powerful tool now,

00:12:28.533 --> 00:12:30.200
we have this reinforcement
learning tool

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:32.420
that we can use
for post-training.

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:35.390
And initially, the
instinct was to say

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.750
there's one true objective,
and that objective

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:39.680
is whether people like the bot.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:42.430
And so if we just optimize
for the true objective we

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:43.905
care about, that's all we need.

00:12:43.905 --> 00:12:46.510
It turns out to
be very difficult.

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.537
Human approval is easy to hack.

00:12:49.537 --> 00:12:50.870
It's hard to collect that scale.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.350
It's hard to RL at scale
for all those reasons you

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:56.480
see over optimization and
all these kinds of issues.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.270
On the other hand,
we could instead

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:01.360
turn to where RL has
really dominated-- things

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:03.110
like AlphaGo or AlphaFold.

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.060
And then looking
at those domains,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.390
you might say,
what we really need

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:10.780
is a domain in which
we know the true reward

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:13.750
and for which we can evaluate
such true rewards very quickly

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:15.080
and efficiently and at scale.

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:17.080
And if we could do
that, then we can

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:19.510
bring to bear all of the
successes of reinforcement

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:22.810
learning from the past
into language modeling.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:26.200
So that's the overall mindset
that we're adopting now.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:27.860
We have the tools
that we've built,

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:32.410
now let's apply them
into a very different way

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:34.750
of using the same tools,
taking inspiration

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:38.001
from successes in RL.

00:13:38.001 --> 00:13:41.500
And so today, I'm going
to talk in two parts.

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:44.170
So the first part is just going
to be an algorithmic part,

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:46.020
so this part on the very top.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.808
I'm going to talk about
different algorithms.

00:13:48.808 --> 00:13:50.850
First, I'm going to talk
about PPO in more detail

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:54.160
because I cut that from last
lecture for length reasons.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:55.980
And then I'm going
to transform PPO

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.380
into GRPO, a simpler
version in some sense.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:01.530
And then we'll talk
through those objectives

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:05.790
and various implementation
details of GRPO

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:10.390
to get us to multiple
canonical variations of GRPO.

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:13.960
And having done that, we will
then walk through the three,

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:17.850
I think, big reasoning
model training things

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.993
that have been done as case
studies, since after part 1,

00:14:20.993 --> 00:14:22.410
you'll have all
the tools that you

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.080
need to understand everything
that is happening in part 2.

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.830
And after you do part
2, you'll understand

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:31.170
at least how these three
Chinese open LLMs were made.

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:34.622
So now we are going
to return to PPO.

00:14:34.622 --> 00:14:36.330
It is the thing that
I didn't want to do,

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:37.747
but we have to do
in order for you

00:14:37.747 --> 00:14:40.730
to understand why GRPO exists.

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:42.440
So what is PPO?

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:45.600
At least in my mental
model of what PPO is,

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.510
we start from the
simplest possible thing,

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:51.380
and then we move slowly but
surely downwards to PPO.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:53.257
So the simplest possible thing--

00:14:53.257 --> 00:14:54.840
I mentioned this in
the last lecture--

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:56.310
this is the policy gradient.

00:14:56.310 --> 00:15:00.020
So on the left, I would like
to optimize the expected reward

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.263
under my policy p of theta.

00:15:02.263 --> 00:15:04.680
And we're going to optimize
that through gradient descent.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:06.330
So I take the gradient
of that object

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.538
and I get the right-hand
side of that equation, which

00:15:08.538 --> 00:15:10.520
is the policy gradient,
the expectation

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.660
under my current
policy of the reward.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.320
And I'm going to take gradient
steps that either increase

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.690
or decrease the
probability according

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:19.560
to the sign of the reward z.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.570
Hopefully, straightforward.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.060
If this is not
familiar to you, you

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:26.900
can brush up on how that policy
gradient derivation happens.

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:29.420
Now, there are
two things that we

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:33.040
might want to think about here
that are kind of inefficient.

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:35.620
So the first thing is
that this is what you

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:37.750
might call purely on policy.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.190
So the way that the
reinforced gradients work,

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.220
I have to sample from
p theta, and then I

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.770
will immediately take a step
on those sampled examples.

00:15:45.770 --> 00:15:48.790
And so every time that I
want to take a gradient step,

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:50.210
I have to compute a reward.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:52.540
And I have to do a rollout.

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:55.880
You will understand this
in your assignment 5,

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.710
but the expansive part
of RL is the rollouts.

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:00.460
We have to actually
run the language model

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.260
and get samples, and
we that that's slow.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:03.722
From Percy's
inference lecture, we

00:16:03.722 --> 00:16:05.139
know that that's
very complicated.

00:16:05.139 --> 00:16:06.250
It's very tricky.

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:09.560
We would much rather
sample less frequently.

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:11.960
We do a rollout
once, we sample once,

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:14.630
and then we take multiple
updates on those rollouts.

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:16.420
So that motivates
something called

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:21.860
TRPO, where instead of
taking updates from p theta,

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:25.190
I would like to allow
my updates to go stale.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:28.120
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to sample from p theta

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:32.360
old, this base distribution
down here, but still get

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:34.050
valid policy gradients.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:35.280
Well, how can I do that?

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:38.130
Well, I can do what's called an
importance sampling correction.

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:41.960
And then I can keep my
policies close to my old one

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.600
so that I don't get
too far from my old one

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:47.180
and get crazy reward estimates.

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:48.450
Very simple idea.

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:51.120
Just do a little bit of
off-to-on policy correction,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:52.580
you get something called TRPO.

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:55.838
This A of t is a lower
variance version of R of z.

00:16:55.838 --> 00:16:57.380
I'm not going to
talk about advantage

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:59.640
estimation in much more detail.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:01.830
PPO is a simple extra step.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.859
Beyond this, it says instead
of doing KL divergences,

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:07.050
I'm just going to
clip the advantages.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.960
And this is naturally going to
force my policy to remain close,

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:12.317
because if I go too
far, then I'm not

00:17:12.317 --> 00:17:14.400
going to be able to get
higher and higher rewards.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.150
These rewards will just
get clipped off at 1

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:18.607
minus epsilon or 1 plus epsilon.

00:17:18.607 --> 00:17:20.940
There's an upper bound to how
much reward I can collect,

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:22.915
so there's no incentive
for the RL algorithm

00:17:22.915 --> 00:17:25.290
to make the policy really
different from the current one.

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:29.320
So the soft version that you
can say think of this idea.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:29.900
OK.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:33.790
So PPO, very successful
RL algorithm,

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:38.470
used in many different
places, used in very toy RL

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:39.590
environments.

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:43.750
It was used in OpenAI
DOTA bot, works very well

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:45.365
in such actual RL tasks.

00:17:45.365 --> 00:17:46.490
Oh yeah, there was a video.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:47.500
I forgot about that.

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:49.990
Look, you can run around.

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:53.440
And at a conceptual level,
it's not that complicated.

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:57.140
If you go look at the OpenAI
documentation for PPO,

00:17:57.140 --> 00:17:58.450
it looks fairly simple.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:00.770
You see that equation
that I listed above,

00:18:00.770 --> 00:18:02.565
it's this PPO-Clip objective.

00:18:02.565 --> 00:18:03.940
And really, the
only thing that's

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:07.090
maybe slightly complicated
is that this A of t

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:09.900
is actually calculated by
using something called a value

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:10.400
function.

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:12.070
So you need a second
neural network

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:15.460
to essentially compute the
expected rewards, and that

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:17.140
will be used to, in
some sense, lower

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:20.021
the variance of my gradients.

00:18:20.021 --> 00:18:23.320
I'm not going to go into the
details once again of all the RL

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:26.350
details, but the one
implementation difference

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:28.600
here that matters is that
we need this value function,

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:32.100
and that will become
important in a moment here.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:35.550
But PPO in practice is a
very, very different beast

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:37.650
from PPO in theory.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.390
And you're in for
a very bad time

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:43.710
if there's a blog post
that says 37 implementation

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:46.110
details of PPO.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:49.140
I don't want to know
all 37 of those, guys.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:55.260
And there's a huge, long list
of different PPO variants

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:56.790
in this blog post,
and all of them

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.980
have different scores
on the RL benchmarks.

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:01.410
And there's a
whole paper written

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:04.413
on why implementation
details matter in PPO

00:19:04.413 --> 00:19:05.830
and how, if you
really mess it up,

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.122
you're not even computing
the policy gradient correctly

00:19:08.122 --> 00:19:09.900
anymore, but it
actually works better.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:14.850
So it's kind of a crazy place if
you look at the implementation

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:16.028
details of PPO.

00:19:16.028 --> 00:19:18.570
So we actually do have to maybe
look at a live implementation

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:19.127
detail.

00:19:19.127 --> 00:19:21.210
I'll actually go over it
quickly because PPO isn't

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:22.810
the point of this lecture.

00:19:22.810 --> 00:19:25.860
But what I want to get
across to you is you

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:27.780
all haven't been
necessarily working

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.030
on this post-training
space, why do people care so

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.930
much about alternatives to PPO?

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:33.570
It is because of
things like this.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.260
PPO works well, but
it is kind of a beast.

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:38.950
And this is one example.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:43.920
I think from the early days
of RLHF reimplementations,

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:45.625
there was this nice
diagram describing

00:19:45.625 --> 00:19:48.000
this is how you would do a
pretty standard implementation

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:48.880
of PPO.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:51.210
This diagram is pretty gnarly.

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:53.572
We've got a reward
model for RLHF.

00:19:53.572 --> 00:19:55.530
We've got a value model
that's supposed to keep

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:57.870
track of the expected reward.

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:00.330
We've got generalized
advantage estimation.

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.250
I haven't even
explained what that is.

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:05.500
And that's going to go into
the policy language model,

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.600
and we're going to do our
policy gradient update.

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:09.690
So there's all
this machinery that

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:13.560
has to go into place
for PPO to work.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:17.730
So we're going to look at an
implementation a few years back

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.230
now, actually.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:22.530
Some of my students and I did
reimplementations of algorithms

00:20:22.530 --> 00:20:24.485
like PPO, and other
people have used it.

00:20:24.485 --> 00:20:26.860
So this is an implementation
that's been somewhat tested.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:28.818
Not to say that this is
a good implementation--

00:20:28.818 --> 00:20:31.920
I'm sure something more
modern implementations

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:35.010
from Volcano Engine and
other things probably

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:37.110
work a little bit better.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:38.760
But I just want to
walk you through all

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:42.510
the different components
also, because as you write

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:45.750
GPU in your assignments,
your structure will probably

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:48.180
mirror some aspects of this.

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:50.920
All RL algorithms have a
similar kind of outer loop.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:54.690
So if we look at
this, so a PPO step

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.690
looks very similar to a
standard gradient update

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:59.380
step in a language model.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:00.708
So this is the outer loop.

00:21:00.708 --> 00:21:02.250
What we're going to
do is we're going

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:04.320
to get a bunch of rollouts.

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.007
And once we get a bunch of
rollouts, what we're going to do

00:21:07.007 --> 00:21:08.590
is we're going to
compute some losses.

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:10.757
And then we're going to
just take backwards and then

00:21:10.757 --> 00:21:13.330
gradient steps with clip
norm, and that's it.

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:19.440
Very unintimidating-- the outer
loop of RL is usually fine.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:22.680
Loss computation is also
basically the same thing.

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:26.630
So this is probably small
for those of you in the back.

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:28.580
But if you look at
this, what you have

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.530
is one block that's computing
the loss for the value function.

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:34.200
Remember PPO has both a
value function and a policy,

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:36.090
so we have to update
both of those at once.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:38.210
So we're computing
how close is the value

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:42.628
function, the actual returns,
the rewards that we saw.

00:21:42.628 --> 00:21:45.170
And we want to keep those close
because the value function is

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:47.460
supposed to provide
variance reduction.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:49.790
And then we've got
the actual rewards

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:52.650
that we're going to
update on for PPO.

00:21:52.650 --> 00:21:54.410
And then we have the
clipping constants

00:21:54.410 --> 00:21:55.920
1 minus epsilon 1 plus epsilon.

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:58.560
This code is literally just
copying this back over.

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:02.750
And just to give you some
intuition for hyperparameters--

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:05.736
a typical cliprange might
be something like 0.2 here.

00:22:05.736 --> 00:22:10.310
So you would be allowed to go
essentially from 0.8 to 1.2

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:15.770
in likelihood ratio between
your old and new policy.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:19.080
The rollouts is where
things start to get gnarly,

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:21.410
but this is still, essentially,
just calling inference.

00:22:21.410 --> 00:22:24.100
The left side is really
just sampling rollouts

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:25.600
from your current
policy, and then

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:27.610
you evaluate the
logprobs of your samples,

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:29.180
and you move on to the right.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:30.350
And what do you see?

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:34.445
Well, the only subtlety here is
that your value and your reward

00:22:34.445 --> 00:22:36.820
and your policy may have
different tokenizers-- so you've

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:37.940
got to do tokenization.

00:22:37.940 --> 00:22:41.520
But otherwise, you just feed
your rollouts into the model.

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:43.270
At this point, you're
looking at this code

00:22:43.270 --> 00:22:44.478
and you say, OK, that's fine.

00:22:44.478 --> 00:22:46.630
This is not so horrible.

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:50.860
But the places where things
start to get a little bit tricky

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:53.240
is first, we have to
do some reward-shaping.

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:55.540
So what does that mean?

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:57.970
So stepping slightly
back, one thing

00:22:57.970 --> 00:23:02.200
that's weird about reinforcement
learning for language models is

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:04.760
if you think about it
from the RL perspective,

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:07.190
technically, it's kind
of a contextual bandit.

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:08.960
Well, what is a
contextual bandit?

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:11.410
A contextual bandit is
something where you get an input

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:14.300
and you have a bunch of
possible actions to take,

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:15.850
and you immediately
get a reward.

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:17.645
In language modeling,
you get a prompt.

00:23:17.645 --> 00:23:20.020
You can give an output, and
you immediately get a reward.

00:23:20.020 --> 00:23:21.292
There's no state transitions.

00:23:21.292 --> 00:23:22.750
There's no environment
exploration.

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:25.140
There's none of this complexity.

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:27.750
Reward shaping
here is essentially

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:31.950
constructing per-token
losses in order

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:35.870
to be able to give you something
more easy to learn for the RL

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:36.370
algorithm.

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:38.310
And so what happens
in practice for PPO

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:40.860
and for GRPO, which
you'll be implementing,

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:43.830
is that the KL terms,
essentially, the regularization

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:46.570
that we're applying is
actually computed per token,

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:49.290
whereas the actual
true reward like the--

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.270
did you complete the
task or not thing,

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:53.050
that's computed at
the very last token.

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:55.380
So you see that
there's essentially

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:57.460
a per-token reward for
the regularization,

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:01.490
and there's a single terminal
reward at the end for success

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:01.990
or not.

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:02.580
Yes.

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:03.720
So what does it mean?

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:06.510
What does not work?

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:07.090
Oh, right.

00:24:09.934 --> 00:24:11.490
This is kind of funny.

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:14.740
Basically, we're only
adding per-token KL penalty.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:18.290
But once again, this is one of
those funny PPO implementation

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:19.100
things.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:23.450
This second line here, this is
the actual true KL calculation.

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:25.860
But when this number
goes to negative,

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:27.578
it becomes numerically unstable.

00:24:27.578 --> 00:24:29.370
And you can actually
hit that pretty often.

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:31.490
And so this gets
clamped off at 0,

00:24:31.490 --> 00:24:36.080
which all of if you clip off
a log likelihood ratio at 0,

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:40.160
that's not a KL anymore, but it
is some approximation to a KL.

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:43.070
Yes.

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:45.120
So we've talked
about loss function.

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:47.345
What are our update
steps, especially

00:24:47.345 --> 00:24:52.470
as it relates to
the reward function?

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:54.630
Is that the same as other--

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:55.210
Yeah.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:55.710
Good point.

00:24:55.710 --> 00:24:58.550
So the updates, as I was
saying in this outer loop,

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:01.380
is actually just
taking gradient steps.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:03.710
So in terms of your code,
it will look no different

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:06.770
than just taking
normal gradient steps.

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:09.170
What is happening here,
though, is in some sense--

00:25:09.170 --> 00:25:12.980
let's go back to the policy
gradient equation here.

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:17.290
If you write down this thing,
this is R of z times p theta z.

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:20.610
This is just taking
gradients with respect

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:23.200
to theta of Rz log p theta z.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:26.232
It's a weighted loss that
you then take gradients of.

00:25:26.232 --> 00:25:27.940
And what you're not
doing is technically,

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:29.020
if you're taking
a real gradient,

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:31.000
you should also take a
gradient into p theta,

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:31.960
but you don't do that.

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:33.880
There's an implicit
stop gradient.

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:36.670
And so all you do is you
compute this inner loss,

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:38.340
you feed it to the
autograd, and you'll

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:42.035
get the right steps for
the policy gradient.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.140
You'll have to do that for
the assignment as well.

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:48.557
And we have a
little tutorial that

00:25:48.557 --> 00:25:50.550
explains what's going
on there in case

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.540
that quick explanation
was not clear.

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:54.480
And finally, the
part that I think

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:57.840
is the gnarliest is there's
the box that I did not

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:00.440
explain called generalized
advantage estimation.

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.050
So what you need to do, whenever
you have a policy gradient,

00:26:04.050 --> 00:26:07.150
the variances of the
gradient are often very high.

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:11.020
And so you want to do as much
variance reduction as you can.

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:14.780
And instead of multiplying
the gradients with the rewards

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:19.010
directly, instead, you can
show that the following A of t

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:23.100
quantity, which is essentially a
discounted advantage estimate--

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:24.710
that's RL term--

00:26:24.710 --> 00:26:26.580
is an appropriate substitute.

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:29.270
And so one of the things that
PPO does that's very different

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:31.250
is to use this advantage
estimate, where

00:26:31.250 --> 00:26:35.270
you can tweak lambda and
gamma to trade off bias

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:38.265
and variance of your gradients.

00:26:38.265 --> 00:26:40.640
One of the funny things,
though, is despite the annoyance

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:42.620
of this implementation
complexity

00:26:42.620 --> 00:26:44.330
of maintaining
this value function

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:46.250
and estimating all
these quantities,

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:48.890
you can just pick gamma
equals lambda equals 1, which

00:26:48.890 --> 00:26:51.680
reduces this whole thing
basically to a baselined policy

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:52.260
gradient.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:56.400
So you're just taking R
minus the implied value.

00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:57.540
And that works well, too.

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:00.170
And so the point of
making you go through this

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:04.520
was a lot of the implementation
details for PPO are both simple

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:06.800
in that it is just
an outer loop RL,

00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:09.890
you do just take gradients,
and also kind of annoying.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:11.638
You have to do all
these clipping things.

00:27:11.638 --> 00:27:12.680
You have to think about--

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:15.310
what am I going to do with the
generalized advantage estimate?

00:27:15.310 --> 00:27:18.520
What am I going to do to
train the value estimate?

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:22.540
But you expect to see overall
increasing rewards, including

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:26.030
reward models and negative
KL rewards, go down.

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:29.318
This is, once again,
a contextual bandit,

00:27:29.318 --> 00:27:31.610
so you expect to see pretty
reasonable training curves,

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:33.910
not like crazy RL ones.

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:37.190
So I went through PPO.

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:38.870
That was kind of
a whirlwind tour,

00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:42.160
but I think hopefully you get
the context of A, what PPO is,

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:44.680
and B, that it is
sometimes a little bit

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:46.430
tricky to get working.

00:27:46.430 --> 00:27:49.480
And so this has
motivated a lot of people

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:52.390
to try to find
alternatives to PPO.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:54.010
What we're going
to want to do is

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:56.980
to apply essentially
these RL algorithms

00:27:56.980 --> 00:27:59.260
to settings where PPO applies.

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:02.080
And PPO applies very well
to general RL settings

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:05.740
where you have rewards,
but we don't want

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:07.100
the complicated implementation.

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:08.610
And maybe more
importantly, we want

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:09.940
to get rid of the value model.

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:12.340
That is actually, if you
try to implement PPO really,

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:14.730
really annoying, because
the value model is usually

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:16.450
as big as your policy.

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:18.720
So now in terms of
GPU memory, you're

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:21.990
paying twice the cost
of your language model.

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:24.880
Now you might say, OK,
why can't we use DPO?

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:28.060
Well, DPO is well-suited
for pairwise comparisons,

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:29.880
like Bradley-Terry comparisons.

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:32.190
It's not so good if
what you want to do

00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:34.890
is, let's say, do reinforcement
learning on math questions

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:36.932
and check whether or not
the answers are correct.

00:28:36.932 --> 00:28:39.790
There's no pairwise structure,
so to speak, inherently there.

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:42.180
So maybe DPO is not great.

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:46.410
DPO also originally is
an offline algorithm,

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:49.393
in a sense, in that it has a
whole bunch of pairs that you

00:28:49.393 --> 00:28:52.060
initially collect, and then you
just update your model on those.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.770
You could make it
online by iterating,

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:58.200
but that's not usually the
way in which people apply DPO.

00:28:58.200 --> 00:29:00.550
So then now this brings
us to the new hotness,

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:02.860
which I think is GRPO.

00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:08.020
So GRPO is actually very, very
simple, both in motivation

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:10.040
and in actual implementation.

00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:13.960
So where you start with is
conceptually you start at PPO.

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:17.050
You start with very
similar pieces.

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:18.890
You think about
this clipping thing.

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:22.750
You think about policy
updates in very similar ways.

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:26.860
But what you do is you
remove the really complicated

00:29:26.860 --> 00:29:28.700
generalized advantage
estimation--

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:30.165
you just get rid
of it completely,

00:29:30.165 --> 00:29:31.540
and you replace
it with something

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:33.640
that is much, much simpler.

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:35.870
So what is the
much simpler thing?

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:39.430
Well, we are going to
replace the advantage, which

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:41.300
used to be this GAA thing.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:44.405
It was the sum over returns and
had the value function in there.

00:29:44.405 --> 00:29:46.030
Instead, it's going
to be this equation

00:29:46.030 --> 00:29:49.040
3 at the bottom of
this slide here.

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:50.380
And what is this?

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:54.400
The advantage of response
I is equal to the reward

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:57.940
that response I receives minus
the mean of the responses

00:29:57.940 --> 00:29:59.237
within my group.

00:29:59.237 --> 00:30:01.070
And I'll define what a
group is in a moment.

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:03.880
And then divide it by
the standard deviation

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:05.920
of the rewards within the group.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:07.740
So this is a
z-score, if you know

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:11.460
what that is, of the
rewards within the group.

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:12.700
Now, what is a group?

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:14.400
A group is a very,
in some sense,

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:17.290
natural object for
our language model RL.

00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:20.220
You have an input question,
let's say, solve this math

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:22.060
problem, that is a group.

00:30:22.060 --> 00:30:24.220
And I have many different
candidate responses.

00:30:24.220 --> 00:30:27.420
I have capital G
different responses,

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:30.370
and those are all the responses
within my single group.

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:33.990
So the nice thing is if
you think about it, maybe

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.750
problems are harder or easier.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:39.180
Some math problems are
much harder than others.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:42.030
And because of that,
the average reward

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:46.270
that my other samples receive is
a natural baseline for myself.

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:49.420
And people have explored exactly
these kinds of algorithms.

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:51.477
If you look up
reinforcement learning

00:30:51.477 --> 00:30:54.060
with leave one out-- or sorry,
policy gradients with leave one

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:56.610
out, this is the
leave one out baseline

00:30:56.610 --> 00:31:00.990
in action minus the standard
deviation piece right here.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:02.730
Yes.

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:04.370
I was wondering about
the truth of what

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:05.870
you're talking about here.

00:31:05.870 --> 00:31:09.290
So you said it's like they
have a batch of questions

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:11.850
and then there are answers
to it, which are rewarded.

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:14.870
So each answer
only corresponding

00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:16.460
to one unique
question, or are we

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:20.600
doing multiple trajectories
of the same question

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:21.865
for multiple questions?

00:31:21.865 --> 00:31:24.530
You do multiple
answers per question.

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:27.380
And that's how you get
variance reduction in this.

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:30.920
It has multiple different
questions and also

00:31:30.920 --> 00:31:33.192
multiple answers for
each of the questions.

00:31:33.192 --> 00:31:34.650
Is that the way to
understand this?

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:35.150
No.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:39.620
So for each question Q, GRPO
samples a group of outputs.

00:31:39.620 --> 00:31:41.120
This is not batched.

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:43.130
Normally, if you were
doing this for real,

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:44.670
you would have
multiple questions,

00:31:44.670 --> 00:31:47.220
and for each question,
you'd have G responses,

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:49.010
and those would get
baselined together.

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:52.313
Across questions, you would have
no baselining or any interaction

00:31:52.313 --> 00:31:54.480
really other than the
policies get updated together.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:58.370
So the baseline is only doing
within the same questions.

00:31:58.370 --> 00:32:00.060
And that's why the
baseline makes sense,

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:02.690
because a question
is hard or easy,

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:04.870
and the mean of that
reward is, in some sense,

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:06.290
capturing the
question difficulty,

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:08.680
and you're subtracting
that guy out.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:10.120
OK, good.

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:12.197
And the other thing--
this is a fun note,

00:32:12.197 --> 00:32:13.780
I'm going to mention
it because I just

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:15.440
think this is fun and cool.

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:19.840
This DKL, if you've seen lots
of KL divergence computations,

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:22.500
this is actually a
little nonstandard.

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:27.280
Because the natural KL
divergence estimate is you

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:28.840
take a bunch of
samples and then you

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:31.000
compute the average of
the log ratio, which

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:32.750
is this inner term over here.

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:38.020
But this one from GRPO actually
has these two extra terms--

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:42.250
it has the ratio of pi ref over
pi theta and has this minus 1.

00:32:42.250 --> 00:32:44.470
And you can convince
yourself that if you

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:48.010
take the expectation of this
with respect to pi theta,

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:52.430
this is just going to be
canceling with this 1 over here.

00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:54.520
So this is a control
variate scheme

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:57.970
that reduces the variance
of this KL divergence

00:32:57.970 --> 00:33:01.260
estimate, which is cool because
maybe you all need to estimate

00:33:01.260 --> 00:33:02.860
KL divergence from samples.

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:05.190
This equation 2 is just
a slightly nicer way

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:09.040
of estimating that
exact same thing.

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:10.900
And GRPO is really nice.

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:12.558
Oh, one last note.

00:33:12.558 --> 00:33:14.100
If you're only taking
one step, which

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:16.410
is doing the pure online case--

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:20.340
all this clipping stuff just
disappears, and all you're doing

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:21.900
is policy gradients.

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:23.820
Policy gradient is just
upweighting good stuff

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:25.320
and downweighting
bad stuff, where

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:28.090
the rewards that I'm multiplying
the policy gradients by

00:33:28.090 --> 00:33:29.170
is this of A of i.

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:32.520
So it's just like an incredibly
simple algorithm for the truly

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:35.400
online case where you're
not doing multiple steps

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:37.500
on a single example.

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:41.370
So there's multiple
different repositories

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:43.620
for GRPO implementations.

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:47.100
I can point you to several,
including this one that I copied

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:49.770
and put onto this slide here.

00:33:49.770 --> 00:33:54.240
But basically, I can just
say that it's exactly

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:55.950
the way you think it would go.

00:33:55.950 --> 00:33:57.830
In the outer loop,
you would compute

00:33:57.830 --> 00:33:59.520
the reward for each rollout.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:02.510
You'd normalize for
each group the mean

00:34:02.510 --> 00:34:04.110
and the variance of the rewards.

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:06.090
You'd compute the KL
term, in this case,

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:08.989
per sequence, which isn't
quite right for the more

00:34:08.989 --> 00:34:10.290
heavyweight implementations.

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:12.210
And then you do gradient
updates on the loss.

00:34:12.210 --> 00:34:15.409
And this is one example
of the loss computation

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:17.570
that you also have here.

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:20.960
And the advantage computation,
unlike in the PPO case,

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:22.679
is just really, really simple.

00:34:22.679 --> 00:34:25.489
This is almost exactly line
for line, the equations

00:34:25.489 --> 00:34:28.159
that I showed you, with just
one minor difference that's

00:34:28.159 --> 00:34:30.350
not shown in the equation.

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:32.900
As you do in almost everything,
because we're dividing

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:36.110
by the standard deviation, you
add a tiny fudge factor of 1e

00:34:36.110 --> 00:34:39.040
negative 4 to make sure it
doesn't numerically blow up

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:39.540
on you.

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:41.748
And you'll have to do this
in the assignment as well.

00:34:41.748 --> 00:34:45.889
You'll have to add a little
epsilon to your GRPO setup.

00:34:45.889 --> 00:34:48.320
So how well does this work?

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:50.690
GRPO works pretty well.

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:54.020
This is from the original
DeepSeek math paper.

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:55.860
And I'll get back to
this later because it

00:34:55.860 --> 00:34:59.640
is interesting to look at
this plot and this result

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:02.080
in light of later
DeepSeek R1 results.

00:35:02.080 --> 00:35:06.090
They show, essentially, the
two fine-tuning-based methods--

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:07.600
RFT and online RFT.

00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:11.400
This is basically a fairly
weak baseline, I would say.

00:35:11.400 --> 00:35:13.590
What you're doing is you're
only looking at examples

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:15.400
that get the right answer.

00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:17.775
They're doing math in this
case, so you only get examples

00:35:17.775 --> 00:35:19.400
where you get the
right answer, and you

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:20.820
fine-tune on your
own outputs that

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:23.520
got the right answer,
reinforcing correct answers

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:25.320
with fine-tuning.

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:27.840
GRPO with outcome-level
rewards where you only

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:30.010
get a correct or not
answer is the yellow.

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:31.930
The blue one is
process-level rewards

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:33.870
where you got a
system that looks

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:36.940
at each step of your reasoning
and gives you a grade for that.

00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:39.193
And they're arguing maybe
process rewards are better.

00:35:39.193 --> 00:35:41.110
We'll talk a little bit
more about that later.

00:35:41.110 --> 00:35:43.170
But in either case,
you see that GGPO works

00:35:43.170 --> 00:35:46.770
and it works pretty well.

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:49.570
Any questions about
the basic GRPO piece

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:52.740
before we move on to details
about GRPO and thinking

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:57.420
deeply about what's actually
happening in the algorithm?

00:35:57.420 --> 00:35:57.920
Good.

00:35:57.920 --> 00:35:58.590
OK.

00:35:58.590 --> 00:36:03.332
So now let's think about the
difference between GRPO and PPO

00:36:03.332 --> 00:36:05.040
and what we've done
and what's different.

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:08.577
So really there's-- as I was
saying, only one difference,

00:36:08.577 --> 00:36:10.410
although it's a really
important difference.

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:12.680
It's replacing the
advantage estimator

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:16.250
with this thing-- the mean
or the z-score, let's say,

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.710
of the rewards.

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:23.570
And so now I'm going to go
back to the policy gradient

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:25.470
theorem or the policy
gradient result.

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:28.620
And I'm going to think
through this result with you.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:35.220
So when we take a policy
gradient update, what can we do?

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:39.645
So I'll just go back
a couple of slides.

00:36:39.645 --> 00:36:41.720
On this slide, we've got--

00:36:41.720 --> 00:36:43.770
right here at the very
top policy gradients.

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:47.580
This is the most basic RL
algorithm that you can do.

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:51.110
You take gradients where you
multiply the log probability

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:52.200
gradients with the reward.

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:54.200
This is something that
I'm always allowed to do.

00:36:54.200 --> 00:36:56.170
This is a mathematical
equivalence.

00:36:56.170 --> 00:36:58.910
Now, one other thing I can
do is called baselining.

00:36:58.910 --> 00:37:03.280
I can take this reward z and
I can subtract any constant,

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:08.240
in fact, any random variable
that doesn't depend on z itself.

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:12.290
And this would still be
a valid policy gradient.

00:37:12.290 --> 00:37:15.160
So this baselining thing is
really important because what

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:16.618
you're going to
try to do is you're

00:37:16.618 --> 00:37:18.970
going to try to subtract
constants that give you lower

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:21.560
variances on this expectation.

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:23.710
So that's called baselining.

00:37:23.710 --> 00:37:28.222
And if we go here,
that's a classic result

00:37:28.222 --> 00:37:29.930
that you can look up
in Sutton and Barto.

00:37:29.930 --> 00:37:32.030
They say, OK, look, we've
got the policy gradient.

00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:34.450
You can subtract out
any baseline b of S,

00:37:34.450 --> 00:37:37.750
because b of S, when we sum
it up across the policy,

00:37:37.750 --> 00:37:40.870
is going to be 0.

00:37:40.870 --> 00:37:42.410
So this is fine.

00:37:42.410 --> 00:37:43.460
We can always baseline.

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:45.350
But let's look at this A of i.

00:37:45.350 --> 00:37:46.990
Is this a baseline?

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:49.760
Well, we're
subtracting the mean.

00:37:49.760 --> 00:37:53.280
And that's a baseline because
all the other rewards are not

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:54.790
dependent on R of i.

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:55.780
So maybe that's OK.

00:37:55.780 --> 00:37:58.120
I mean, technically, this
notation includes R of I,

00:37:58.120 --> 00:38:00.362
but if I remove that,
that's a valid baseline.

00:38:00.362 --> 00:38:02.070
But one thing that's
really weird, as I'm

00:38:02.070 --> 00:38:04.355
dividing by the
standard deviation here,

00:38:04.355 --> 00:38:06.330
that's not something
that really seems

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:11.670
allowed according to this
derivation in Sutton and Barto.

00:38:11.670 --> 00:38:14.010
And that turns out
to be a problem.

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:17.160
Some folks that have
gone and reanalyzed

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:19.770
GRPO and its behaviors
basically argue

00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:23.530
that GRPO has two things
that at least mathematically

00:38:23.530 --> 00:38:25.260
are a little bit off.

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:28.620
And the first thing is this
division by standard deviation.

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:30.730
As I was talking you
through just now,

00:38:30.730 --> 00:38:34.140
this breaks that contract
that a baseline just

00:38:34.140 --> 00:38:39.120
needs to be subtracting
a 0 mean variable that's

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:42.000
independent of my draw.

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:44.590
And the other thing
that GRPO does,

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:48.310
which I glossed over when
I previously presented it,

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:52.540
is that it's actually
dividing the rewards

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:55.390
by the length of the output.

00:38:55.390 --> 00:38:56.590
And that's going to have--

00:38:56.590 --> 00:38:58.132
that's also a little bit weird.

00:38:58.132 --> 00:38:59.840
According to the policy
gradient theorem,

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:02.980
this is not something that
would naturally show up.

00:39:02.980 --> 00:39:06.850
And so these authors, who did
this pretty interesting study

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:10.223
of GPRO algorithms, argued
that maybe we should just

00:39:10.223 --> 00:39:11.390
get rid of these two things.

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:13.390
And if you do, then
you'll actually

00:39:13.390 --> 00:39:15.550
have much shorter
output length and higher

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:20.410
reward without having
much longer responses.

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:23.462
And so let's talk
about these results

00:39:23.462 --> 00:39:26.710
carefully for each one
of these two fixes.

00:39:26.710 --> 00:39:28.600
And hopefully, by
talking through these,

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:31.760
you will gain an intuition about
how the RL algorithm works.

00:39:31.760 --> 00:39:36.340
So first, I want to talk
about the standard deviation.

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:40.050
This one's maybe somewhat
obvious what it's doing.

00:39:40.050 --> 00:39:40.550
OK.

00:39:40.550 --> 00:39:42.300
Let me go back because
I think it's easier

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:44.740
to talk about when I'm
highlighting the equation here.

00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:47.942
So I'm dividing the advantage
by the standard deviation.

00:39:47.942 --> 00:39:48.900
So what does that mean?

00:39:48.900 --> 00:39:50.980
When the standard
deviation is small,

00:39:50.980 --> 00:39:52.660
the reward is going
to be amplified.

00:39:52.660 --> 00:39:55.680
It's going to be more important
for me to optimize that group

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:57.550
when the standard
deviations are small.

00:39:57.550 --> 00:40:00.070
And when is the standard
deviation small?

00:40:00.070 --> 00:40:02.040
Well, it's when the
problem is too easy

00:40:02.040 --> 00:40:04.470
or it's too hard because
that's when the rewards are

00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:06.610
either all 0 or they're all 1.

00:40:06.610 --> 00:40:09.300
And so there's a bias in
the standard deviation

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:13.050
term that upweights problems
that are too easy or too hard.

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:15.510
The authors argue this
slows convergence.

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:16.115
Maybe true.

00:40:16.115 --> 00:40:18.240
At the very least, it
certainly breaks the validity

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:19.450
of the policy gradient.

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:22.000
The second thing, which is
subtle but also interesting,

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:23.970
is the length normalization.

00:40:23.970 --> 00:40:26.290
Now, let's look at
what's happening here.

00:40:26.290 --> 00:40:28.230
So we have this
length normalization

00:40:28.230 --> 00:40:30.100
before the GPRO reward.

00:40:30.100 --> 00:40:31.270
Now, what does that do?

00:40:31.270 --> 00:40:34.990
If my model got a question
wrong, then the best thing to do

00:40:34.990 --> 00:40:35.490
is--

00:40:35.490 --> 00:40:38.073
I'm receiving negative reward
in here, so the best thing to do

00:40:38.073 --> 00:40:40.685
is to make the response
really, really long.

00:40:40.685 --> 00:40:42.810
And if I get the answer
right, the best thing to do

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:46.860
is to make the answer short so I
can maximize my positive reward.

00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:50.930
So what this does is it actually
produces a model that biases

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:52.457
as aggressively as possible.

00:40:52.457 --> 00:40:54.540
If the model thinks it
can't get the answer right,

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:57.060
it just produces the
longest possible response,

00:40:57.060 --> 00:40:59.150
which is a very, very
bad incentive for you

00:40:59.150 --> 00:41:00.470
to give the model.

00:41:00.470 --> 00:41:02.810
And so if you fix
this, what happens

00:41:02.810 --> 00:41:06.500
is on various toy tasks
like GSM8K and so on,

00:41:06.500 --> 00:41:09.840
you can get a reward
that's just as good.

00:41:09.840 --> 00:41:11.380
The red one is the
modified version,

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:13.130
but the output length
doesn't keep growing

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:14.130
and growing and growing.

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:16.482
It stabilizes at
a certain point.

00:41:16.482 --> 00:41:19.340
And so there is actually
some interesting observation

00:41:19.340 --> 00:41:22.910
that maybe some of the really
long CoTs that people are seeing

00:41:22.910 --> 00:41:28.040
in things like GPRO are a result
of these actual implementation

00:41:28.040 --> 00:41:31.880
details and choices, rather
than inherently long CoTs

00:41:31.880 --> 00:41:35.340
being a necessary part of the
performance of these models.

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:37.970
And I think that's very
interesting, although not fully

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:41.510
proven out class of hypotheses.

00:41:41.510 --> 00:41:42.020
Cool.

00:41:42.020 --> 00:41:42.660
OK.

00:41:42.660 --> 00:41:44.338
So that's the GPRO algorithm.

00:41:44.338 --> 00:41:46.130
Hopefully, now you're
all familiar with it,

00:41:46.130 --> 00:41:47.755
and I think you now
have the background

00:41:47.755 --> 00:41:50.170
to go through all three
of these papers now--

00:41:50.170 --> 00:41:53.380
DeepSeek R1, Kimi
1.5, and Qwen 3.

00:41:53.380 --> 00:41:56.920
Any questions?

00:41:56.920 --> 00:41:57.640
Yes.

00:41:57.640 --> 00:41:59.350
So a question
about why operating

00:41:59.350 --> 00:42:01.820
is bad, just to confirm
my understanding.

00:42:01.820 --> 00:42:04.695
I guess it's bad because
either it's the very easy

00:42:04.695 --> 00:42:06.070
or the very hard
cases, you don't

00:42:06.070 --> 00:42:09.520
want to actually aggressively
update or read them all.

00:42:09.520 --> 00:42:15.490
Yeah, I think this gets into
wishy-washy folk theorem

00:42:15.490 --> 00:42:16.190
territory.

00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:17.607
But actually, some
of these papers

00:42:17.607 --> 00:42:19.040
will talk about
this folk theory.

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:21.220
And so I'll mention it,
which is what you really

00:42:21.220 --> 00:42:24.220
want the RL algorithm
to do is to get problems

00:42:24.220 --> 00:42:25.850
that it can do somewhat well on.

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:28.540
It can get some reward
on, but it's not so easy

00:42:28.540 --> 00:42:29.870
that it can already solve them.

00:42:29.870 --> 00:42:31.390
So there's a
curriculum effect where

00:42:31.390 --> 00:42:34.790
you want to feed the models
the right level of difficulty.

00:42:34.790 --> 00:42:38.120
And if you're maximizing
that standard deviation,

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:39.543
that's the wrong direction.

00:42:39.543 --> 00:42:40.960
You're really
maxing out the stuff

00:42:40.960 --> 00:42:44.120
at the extremes that you either
already all know or just way too

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:45.175
hard for you to solve.

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:48.620
Cool.

00:42:48.620 --> 00:42:49.320
OK.

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:52.340
So we're going to talk about all
three of these papers today--

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:55.734
R1, Kimi 1.5, and Qwen 3.

00:42:55.734 --> 00:42:59.480
R1 and K 1.5 I think are pretty
interesting because they came

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:01.110
out at roughly the same time.

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:05.000
Sadly, R1 was the only one
to get a gigantic social--

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:05.970
what's it called?

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:06.980
Reception.

00:43:06.980 --> 00:43:10.010
But both of these
actually show how

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:14.480
to do RL-based reasoning on
math and other things with LLMs.

00:43:14.480 --> 00:43:17.060
And because they're
contemporaneous,

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:19.710
you can see almost
two parallel ways

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:21.960
to tackle the same problem--
which things are similar,

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:24.500
which things are different,
and so on, which is great.

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:28.190
Qwen 3 is the newest
of the releases.

00:43:28.190 --> 00:43:30.860
And they do some fairly
interesting variations

00:43:30.860 --> 00:43:32.810
of ideas in R1.

00:43:32.810 --> 00:43:37.400
And also they have some new kind
of tricks that R1 doesn't have,

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:39.710
which I think are pretty
interesting things to look at,

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:41.418
especially if you're
interested in things

00:43:41.418 --> 00:43:46.060
like inference efficiency
of reasoning models.

00:43:46.060 --> 00:43:48.790
So I'll start with R1.

00:43:48.790 --> 00:43:52.090
I think R1 is
amazing in the sense

00:43:52.090 --> 00:43:55.390
that it's an arXiv
paper that launched

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:56.870
a whole social phenomenon.

00:43:56.870 --> 00:43:59.770
Never let your advisors tell
you that your arXiv papers

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:00.590
will never matter.

00:44:00.590 --> 00:44:05.930
This one lost like almost half
a billion of NVIDIA valuation.

00:44:05.930 --> 00:44:09.790
You too can one day maybe
cause that kind of a wave.

00:44:09.790 --> 00:44:14.860
R1, I think is quite
remarkable because in many ways

00:44:14.860 --> 00:44:17.920
replicates all of the
qualitative properties

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:21.910
of the o1 recipe in a way
that is extremely simple.

00:44:21.910 --> 00:44:24.220
So the key properties
that I want

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:28.180
to talk through and make
sure you all understand,

00:44:28.180 --> 00:44:32.410
the first thing is that it
hits the performance targets

00:44:32.410 --> 00:44:33.740
that OpenAI o1 set.

00:44:33.740 --> 00:44:35.930
Everyone was really excited
about reasoning models.

00:44:35.930 --> 00:44:37.160
So this is very exciting.

00:44:37.160 --> 00:44:40.050
The second thing
is that it opens up

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:43.870
an RL recipe that is not
only just a replicable one,

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:47.010
but I think more importantly,
one that is extremely simple.

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:48.310
It doesn't have any search.

00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:50.080
It doesn't have any
process reward models.

00:44:50.080 --> 00:44:51.660
I think lots of
people at the time

00:44:51.660 --> 00:44:54.420
thought maybe we need all
these complicated pieces

00:44:54.420 --> 00:44:56.070
to get reasoning models--

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:58.290
R1 really shows you
don't need any of that.

00:44:58.290 --> 00:45:01.380
And then finally, there's
lots of interesting insights

00:45:01.380 --> 00:45:05.160
about the interaction between
supervised fine-tuning and RL

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:09.310
that I think continue
to be really important.

00:45:09.310 --> 00:45:14.430
So the starting point of R1
is they build on DeepSeekMath.

00:45:14.430 --> 00:45:17.490
And actually, some of the
equations I showed you of GRPO

00:45:17.490 --> 00:45:21.390
are from DeepSeekMath, where
they originally proposed GRPO

00:45:21.390 --> 00:45:24.840
as an alternative, simpler,
or more system-efficient

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:25.740
variant of PPO.

00:45:25.740 --> 00:45:28.035
To them, actually, the
most important piece

00:45:28.035 --> 00:45:29.910
was they wanted to get
rid of the value model

00:45:29.910 --> 00:45:33.120
just because it's really
annoying to have around.

00:45:33.120 --> 00:45:35.580
But one thing that's
really interesting

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:38.280
is they actually go for this
yellow line, the outcome

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:40.530
supervision, which is not
actually the best-performing

00:45:40.530 --> 00:45:42.490
model in DeepSeekMath.

00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:45.150
I'll talk about that again, at
the very end of this section

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:46.140
here.

00:45:46.140 --> 00:45:49.240
So I'll walk through all
the different pieces of R1.

00:45:49.240 --> 00:45:52.170
So I'll start with R1
zero, which I think

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:53.440
of as the controlled setting.

00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:58.120
So R1 zero is a very
pure form of RL learning.

00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:00.840
It basically takes
essentially the model

00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:04.140
that is pretrained plus
mid-trained before doing

00:46:04.140 --> 00:46:07.080
any RLHF or instruction
tuning and then throws it

00:46:07.080 --> 00:46:09.670
into the math RL loop.

00:46:09.670 --> 00:46:12.372
And then they try to find
out how well does that do.

00:46:12.372 --> 00:46:16.060
So the details here, how do they
do the reinforcement learning?

00:46:16.060 --> 00:46:19.450
Well, they have a bunch
of math-ish tasks.

00:46:19.450 --> 00:46:21.120
The data is not public.

00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:24.750
They take DeepSeek V3
as their base model.

00:46:24.750 --> 00:46:27.960
And then their rewards,
there's two forms of rewards

00:46:27.960 --> 00:46:28.600
that they use.

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:30.160
One of them is an
accuracy reward.

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:32.200
So did it get the
math question correct?

00:46:32.200 --> 00:46:33.240
Is it correct or not?

00:46:33.240 --> 00:46:34.810
Reward is binary.

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:38.440
They have a format reward that
basically forces the model

00:46:38.440 --> 00:46:41.470
to put its CoT within
thinking tags--

00:46:41.470 --> 00:46:43.160
start thinking, end
of thinking tags.

00:46:43.160 --> 00:46:45.010
And that's important
if you want your model

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:48.910
to have these long
CoTs being used.

00:46:48.910 --> 00:46:51.430
Now, the format reward
feels like something

00:46:51.430 --> 00:46:52.460
that doesn't matter.

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:55.190
But for many papers and from
having talked to many people,

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:57.040
apparently, it is a
pretty critical part

00:46:57.040 --> 00:47:01.970
of actually getting this whole
reasoning RL thing to work.

00:47:01.970 --> 00:47:04.180
Once you do this,
all they're doing

00:47:04.180 --> 00:47:06.650
is doing RL on top
of the base model.

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:09.650
Nothing very fancy, but the
results are pretty striking.

00:47:09.650 --> 00:47:13.120
They get performance that is
getting pretty close to OpenAI

00:47:13.120 --> 00:47:17.470
o1 by just doing some
RL on top of the model

00:47:17.470 --> 00:47:22.300
that they already had
without any CoT fine-tuning

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:24.160
or anything like this.

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:27.580
And there's two things that
they know in their paper

00:47:27.580 --> 00:47:30.395
as being really
interesting about R1 zero.

00:47:30.395 --> 00:47:32.020
And I want to talk
about this because I

00:47:32.020 --> 00:47:34.340
think it's important
to carefully examine

00:47:34.340 --> 00:47:36.243
what is happening in R1 zero.

00:47:36.243 --> 00:47:37.910
So the first thing
they say is, oh, it's

00:47:37.910 --> 00:47:40.190
very cool that if you
just let the model do

00:47:40.190 --> 00:47:43.490
RL on these verifiable
rewards, the length of the CoT

00:47:43.490 --> 00:47:46.430
just increases
pretty predictably.

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:49.550
And in commentary that
I don't necessarily

00:47:49.550 --> 00:47:51.420
agree with, in the
paper, they're like, oh,

00:47:51.420 --> 00:47:53.270
it's learning to solve
harder and harder problems

00:47:53.270 --> 00:47:54.540
by thinking harder and harder.

00:47:54.540 --> 00:47:56.960
It's like, well, maybe.

00:47:56.960 --> 00:47:59.450
They also point out
it's kind of cool

00:47:59.450 --> 00:48:02.350
that they learn phenomena
like backtracking.

00:48:02.350 --> 00:48:03.600
They call this the aha moment.

00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:07.400
I think much has been made
about this in public discourse

00:48:07.400 --> 00:48:10.760
that, wow, it's cool that
RL training can give models

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:12.530
these kinds of
emergent insights.

00:48:12.530 --> 00:48:15.200
I'll refer you back
to the Dr. GRPO

00:48:15.200 --> 00:48:18.590
paper, the one that was talking
about the corrections to GRPO.

00:48:18.590 --> 00:48:21.708
And I think they have, honestly,
pretty good and interesting

00:48:21.708 --> 00:48:23.750
arguments that both of
these are not particularly

00:48:23.750 --> 00:48:24.960
interesting phenomena.

00:48:24.960 --> 00:48:26.870
First of all, they argue
that the length just

00:48:26.870 --> 00:48:28.537
goes up because of
the biased objective,

00:48:28.537 --> 00:48:31.360
not because it's an
inherently interesting object.

00:48:31.360 --> 00:48:33.370
And second, they argue,
well, if you just

00:48:33.370 --> 00:48:36.230
run DeepSeek V3 on a
bunch of math questions,

00:48:36.230 --> 00:48:37.690
you'll also sometimes
output things

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:40.270
like, aha, I can do
this or that, which

00:48:40.270 --> 00:48:45.190
is maybe not like a deeply new
phenomenon that arises from RL.

00:48:45.190 --> 00:48:48.880
Both of these seem like,
given more recent evidence,

00:48:48.880 --> 00:48:52.270
credible things that maybe
there's nothing like emergent

00:48:52.270 --> 00:48:54.640
and special about R1
zero, but it is actually

00:48:54.640 --> 00:48:55.480
working very well.

00:48:55.480 --> 00:48:57.760
It is a good math model.

00:48:57.760 --> 00:49:01.303
So R1 zero, you can think
of as a research setting.

00:49:01.303 --> 00:49:02.720
They're taking a
controlled model.

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:03.790
They're doing something
very controlled

00:49:03.790 --> 00:49:05.540
on top of it, which is math RL.

00:49:05.540 --> 00:49:07.095
And they get a good model out.

00:49:07.095 --> 00:49:10.240
But if you're trying to build a
really strong model that you're

00:49:10.240 --> 00:49:13.330
going to ship to the world, this
is not where you're going to do.

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:15.460
You're going to
basically do everything

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:18.530
that you can to get the
best model that you can.

00:49:18.530 --> 00:49:21.590
So what would you do in that
more unrestricted setting?

00:49:21.590 --> 00:49:24.490
Well, you're going
to maybe insert

00:49:24.490 --> 00:49:26.030
some supervised fine-tuning.

00:49:26.030 --> 00:49:30.085
You're going to take CoTs
from some undisclosed source,

00:49:30.085 --> 00:49:32.460
and you're going to fine-tune
your DeepSeek model on that

00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:33.910
before you do your RL.

00:49:33.910 --> 00:49:35.910
And after you do that,
you don't want your model

00:49:35.910 --> 00:49:39.250
to be like this math savant
that can't do anything else.

00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:42.102
So you're going to apply your
usual post-training pipeline

00:49:42.102 --> 00:49:43.560
on top of that to
make sure that it

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:46.140
can do all the other
tasks that people normally

00:49:46.140 --> 00:49:48.270
want to use these models for.

00:49:48.270 --> 00:49:51.730
So this is the
pipeline differences.

00:49:51.730 --> 00:49:55.080
And so the key difference,
both within the pipeline

00:49:55.080 --> 00:49:59.310
and within the RL, is they
do SFT initialization to try

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:02.940
to get the model to know how to
do long CoTs without starting

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:03.730
with RL.

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:06.580
They add a language
consistency reward

00:50:06.580 --> 00:50:08.580
in order to make sure
that the chains of thought

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:10.470
remain in a single
language, and then they

00:50:10.470 --> 00:50:15.240
do a secondary RF
stage at the end.

00:50:15.240 --> 00:50:17.202
So I think this
makes a lot of sense.

00:50:17.202 --> 00:50:18.660
Every time you want
to do something

00:50:18.660 --> 00:50:20.393
advanced like
reinforcement learning,

00:50:20.393 --> 00:50:22.560
you're probably going to
start by doing a little bit

00:50:22.560 --> 00:50:24.270
of supervised fine-tuning.

00:50:24.270 --> 00:50:27.860
And so even in reasoning
models or long CoT models

00:50:27.860 --> 00:50:29.870
like DeepSeek R1,
this is the case.

00:50:29.870 --> 00:50:34.100
You start with long CoT
supervised fine-tuning data,

00:50:34.100 --> 00:50:37.460
and then you're going to do RL.

00:50:37.460 --> 00:50:40.340
I will point out
that the description

00:50:40.340 --> 00:50:42.995
of where they get this
data and what this data is

00:50:42.995 --> 00:50:44.760
remains very, very vague.

00:50:44.760 --> 00:50:48.830
They don't tell us where is
the CoT data derived from,

00:50:48.830 --> 00:50:50.340
how did they filter it.

00:50:50.340 --> 00:50:54.950
I don't really have any idea
based on reading the R1 paper.

00:50:54.950 --> 00:50:59.120
The claimed benefit of this
is that if you CoT the model--

00:50:59.120 --> 00:51:03.210
sorry, if you SFT the model on
long, let's say English CoTs,

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:05.600
then this gives you an
interpretability benefit.

00:51:05.600 --> 00:51:08.490
As you do RL, you're not
going to get weird gibberish.

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:10.790
It's going to keep the
model closer to these more

00:51:10.790 --> 00:51:14.690
interpretable CoTs that
you started out with,

00:51:14.690 --> 00:51:16.860
and that would be
good for users.

00:51:16.860 --> 00:51:18.950
As you're using a
math model, it would

00:51:18.950 --> 00:51:22.700
be nice to see its
reasoning as it goes.

00:51:22.700 --> 00:51:26.630
And an additional thing, so
when they do SFT initialization,

00:51:26.630 --> 00:51:27.680
they use a ton of data.

00:51:27.680 --> 00:51:31.880
But one really interesting thing
is that for a lot of models,

00:51:31.880 --> 00:51:36.610
even a tiny amount of SFT
on these kinds of long CoT

00:51:36.610 --> 00:51:37.780
data can be good.

00:51:37.780 --> 00:51:39.560
One thing that
some of my students

00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:41.530
in collaboration with Percy--

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:45.340
we did was basically
take a bunch of long CoTs

00:51:45.340 --> 00:51:49.370
from Gemini 2.0 Flash Thinking
and fine-tune Qwen 2.5.

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:52.900
And maybe surprisingly,
with just 1,000 examples,

00:51:52.900 --> 00:51:58.240
you get really, really high math
benchmark accuracy with just

00:51:58.240 --> 00:52:00.530
a little bit of long
CoT fine-tuning.

00:52:00.530 --> 00:52:03.070
So I think both of these are
really pointing to the fact

00:52:03.070 --> 00:52:07.060
that the base model already has
a lot of thinking capabilities

00:52:07.060 --> 00:52:11.720
that you're just priming and
extracting from the model.

00:52:11.720 --> 00:52:14.830
And after that, of course,
you're going to do RL.

00:52:14.830 --> 00:52:18.445
So after you've gotten
the model set up with SFT,

00:52:18.445 --> 00:52:21.320
as with the instruction
tuning and RLHF pipeline.

00:52:21.320 --> 00:52:22.930
So you start with
SFT and then you

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:25.820
do RL to basically get the
model to actually optimize

00:52:25.820 --> 00:52:27.770
the rewards you're looking for.

00:52:27.770 --> 00:52:30.690
The RL part is basically
the same as R1 zero--

00:52:30.690 --> 00:52:33.410
not huge differences, but
with a minor difference

00:52:33.410 --> 00:52:37.200
that you're going to add a
language consistency loss.

00:52:37.200 --> 00:52:39.277
I think this note is
pretty interesting.

00:52:39.277 --> 00:52:41.360
This is a minor note, but
I'll describe it anyway,

00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:43.820
where they basically say--

00:52:43.820 --> 00:52:46.010
they add this language
consistency reward

00:52:46.010 --> 00:52:48.320
because during the training
process, if they just

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:50.900
let the model RL, they
find that actually the CoT

00:52:50.900 --> 00:52:52.130
will language mix.

00:52:52.130 --> 00:52:54.230
It will switch
between languages.

00:52:54.230 --> 00:52:57.740
And if many of you have seen
people playing with reasoning

00:52:57.740 --> 00:53:00.470
models, I've seen people
on the internet post things

00:53:00.470 --> 00:53:04.070
like, oh, it's weird that
Grok 3, like suddenly

00:53:04.070 --> 00:53:05.790
switches to Chinese in the CoT.

00:53:05.790 --> 00:53:08.090
And this is consistent
with those kinds of things

00:53:08.090 --> 00:53:10.730
that if you aggressively
RL the model, actually,

00:53:10.730 --> 00:53:14.252
there's natural tendencies
for models to language mix

00:53:14.252 --> 00:53:15.960
rather than staying
in a single language.

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:18.260
So it actually requires
an additional reward

00:53:18.260 --> 00:53:22.210
to keep it in the
single language.

00:53:22.210 --> 00:53:24.520
And then finally,
after you've done

00:53:24.520 --> 00:53:27.680
RL on math and other
verifiable domains,

00:53:27.680 --> 00:53:30.290
you basically layer on
the usual post-training.

00:53:30.290 --> 00:53:32.560
So you do instruction
tuning and then

00:53:32.560 --> 00:53:36.110
you do the pairwise
preference tuning afterwards.

00:53:36.110 --> 00:53:40.810
So they do an SFTP step where
they combine both reasoning data

00:53:40.810 --> 00:53:44.260
on nonverifiable tasks, like
write a proof of something.

00:53:44.260 --> 00:53:47.620
These are not verifiable,
so they use their own model

00:53:47.620 --> 00:53:50.200
as a judge for whether or not
they got the answer correct.

00:53:50.200 --> 00:53:53.920
They have nonreasoning data
like write a nice essay.

00:53:53.920 --> 00:53:56.410
And they use the same
SFT data set as what

00:53:56.410 --> 00:53:58.810
they used for DeepSeek V3.

00:53:58.810 --> 00:54:01.360
And then finally, for
RLHF, they actually

00:54:01.360 --> 00:54:04.460
still use GRPO for
RLHF, which is cool.

00:54:04.460 --> 00:54:07.150
They use the same RL
algorithm for everything.

00:54:07.150 --> 00:54:11.780
And then they basically just
follow the V3 RLHF pipeline.

00:54:11.780 --> 00:54:13.330
There's not really
anything different

00:54:13.330 --> 00:54:15.700
for this post-training part.

00:54:15.700 --> 00:54:17.500
How well does it work?

00:54:17.500 --> 00:54:20.160
It works very, very
well, I think many of you

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:21.990
probably experienced
this as well.

00:54:21.990 --> 00:54:24.390
R1 was in many ways
a shock because it

00:54:24.390 --> 00:54:26.880
matched the o1
performance really

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:30.120
across the board on
a very simple recipe.

00:54:30.120 --> 00:54:33.090
As I describe this, I don't
think any of you found any of it

00:54:33.090 --> 00:54:38.580
particularly surprising, but the
outcomes speak for themselves.

00:54:38.580 --> 00:54:41.130
You've got on the
English tasks, basically,

00:54:41.130 --> 00:54:44.850
tied or matching o1 on
across the board, really.

00:54:44.850 --> 00:54:47.910
Slightly worse on code models,
but very, very close really

00:54:47.910 --> 00:54:51.270
across all of these
different tasks.

00:54:51.270 --> 00:54:54.450
The final thing that
the R1 paper showed

00:54:54.450 --> 00:54:57.240
is that you can take
these big, big models

00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:59.830
and you can distill
them into other models.

00:54:59.830 --> 00:55:02.350
So you take your
big DeepSeek R1,

00:55:02.350 --> 00:55:04.320
and you can take those
chains of thought.

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:06.820
In their case, they take almost
a million chains of thought,

00:55:06.820 --> 00:55:09.940
and then they fine-tune Qwen
with those chains of thought.

00:55:09.940 --> 00:55:13.710
And they actually get big boosts
in math performance relative

00:55:13.710 --> 00:55:16.590
to the base model, which is
only getting something 50%

00:55:16.590 --> 00:55:19.460
performance on AIME
for the 32B model.

00:55:19.460 --> 00:55:22.520
So they get 25% plus
boost on this task,

00:55:22.520 --> 00:55:25.850
which is pretty surprising.

00:55:25.850 --> 00:55:26.690
Cool.

00:55:26.690 --> 00:55:31.700
And then finally, there's two,
I think, interesting and good

00:55:31.700 --> 00:55:33.390
observations from R1.

00:55:33.390 --> 00:55:35.420
And I think
scientifically, maybe this

00:55:35.420 --> 00:55:38.040
was the biggest
contribution of R1 in a way.

00:55:38.040 --> 00:55:40.700
So I think R1 maybe had three
contributions scientifically.

00:55:40.700 --> 00:55:44.840
One of it was it showed that
outcome-based rewards with GRPO

00:55:44.840 --> 00:55:45.420
work.

00:55:45.420 --> 00:55:46.950
It's the positive proof.

00:55:46.950 --> 00:55:51.900
And then R1 also had two other
negative results contributions.

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:56.160
And they are contained in the
very last part of the R1 report.

00:55:56.160 --> 00:55:59.540
And they basically say, OK,
we tried two things pretty

00:55:59.540 --> 00:56:00.180
extensively.

00:56:00.180 --> 00:56:05.000
We tried PRMs and we also tried
MCTS, and neither of those

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:09.410
really helped us at all on
replicating something like o1.

00:56:09.410 --> 00:56:11.630
And so to get into a
little bit of detail,

00:56:11.630 --> 00:56:15.090
PRMs are basically
process-reward models.

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:18.540
Those are systems that can
give you intermediate rewards

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:19.350
on a proof.

00:56:19.350 --> 00:56:21.580
So when your model is
giving a chain of thought,

00:56:21.580 --> 00:56:23.640
a PRM would be able to
say, oh, you went wrong

00:56:23.640 --> 00:56:25.240
at this step in the middle.

00:56:25.240 --> 00:56:27.960
And obviously, that is a
much richer and very powerful

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:28.950
form of feedback.

00:56:28.950 --> 00:56:32.430
An RL algorithm can make really,
really good use of a PRM.

00:56:32.430 --> 00:56:34.350
But unfortunately, it's
also very difficult

00:56:34.350 --> 00:56:36.510
to get a PRM in the first place.

00:56:36.510 --> 00:56:40.140
And so R1 in the
DeepSeekMath, people,

00:56:40.140 --> 00:56:44.380
they had gone down this road
of doing PRM for a while.

00:56:44.380 --> 00:56:46.380
And they concluded this
doesn't work quite as

00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:47.678
well as outcome-based rewards.

00:56:47.678 --> 00:56:49.470
And thus far, I think
outcome-based rewards

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:52.770
remain the way in which you
would build these models.

00:56:52.770 --> 00:56:56.370
The second thing that I think
hasn't really panned out

00:56:56.370 --> 00:56:57.880
is search-based method.

00:56:57.880 --> 00:56:59.580
I think lots of
people were interested

00:56:59.580 --> 00:57:02.798
in search-based approaches to
reasoning-- thus far, at least,

00:57:02.798 --> 00:57:04.590
it hasn't really panned
out in the same way

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:07.660
that RL and outcome-based
rewards has.

00:57:07.660 --> 00:57:12.420
That remains, I think, the
strongest baseline and system

00:57:12.420 --> 00:57:14.450
in this universe.

00:57:14.450 --> 00:57:15.190
OK.

00:57:15.190 --> 00:57:18.950
So any questions about
R1, their setup or any

00:57:18.950 --> 00:57:19.890
of the other findings?

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:21.278
Yes.

00:57:21.278 --> 00:57:23.990
[INAUDIBLE]

00:57:23.990 --> 00:57:27.180
The GRPO and PRM are
two different things.

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:28.380
[INAUDIBLE]

00:57:28.380 --> 00:57:28.880
Oh, yeah.

00:57:28.880 --> 00:57:29.250
That's right.

00:57:29.250 --> 00:57:29.390
Yes.

00:57:29.390 --> 00:57:29.600
OK.

00:57:29.600 --> 00:57:29.880
Good.

00:57:29.880 --> 00:57:30.980
Sorry, I said I was
going to mention it

00:57:30.980 --> 00:57:32.910
but I didn't, so that
is totally on me.

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:33.480
That's right.

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:34.490
Exactly.

00:57:34.490 --> 00:57:37.640
And so especially
for the PRM, I think

00:57:37.640 --> 00:57:41.660
it's really interesting and
telling that in DeepSeekMath,

00:57:41.660 --> 00:57:45.720
they were very much convinced
by the strength of the PRM,

00:57:45.720 --> 00:57:47.280
which is this blue line with PS.

00:57:47.280 --> 00:57:51.500
And then in R1, they concluded
that actually this approach that

00:57:51.500 --> 00:57:55.110
had worked for DeepSeekMath was
not really going to work for R1,

00:57:55.110 --> 00:57:58.530
and they had gone with
outcome-based rewards.

00:57:58.530 --> 00:57:59.910
Thank you for reminding me.

00:57:59.910 --> 00:58:02.750
I was about to forget
my promise there.

00:58:02.750 --> 00:58:03.920
Yes.

00:58:03.920 --> 00:58:08.040
So my understanding for
CoT is that for, I guess,

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:11.060
ease of understanding,
or do they [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:11.060 --> 00:58:12.860
how it impacts the performance.

00:58:12.860 --> 00:58:14.242
Yeah.

00:58:14.242 --> 00:58:15.200
It's in this note here.

00:58:15.200 --> 00:58:18.040
They basically say, if you
ablate away the language

00:58:18.040 --> 00:58:20.020
consistency
experiment, it results

00:58:20.020 --> 00:58:22.210
in degradation of the
model's performance,

00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:24.520
but they're going
to put it in anyway

00:58:24.520 --> 00:58:26.800
because they prefer
to have CoTs that

00:58:26.800 --> 00:58:28.720
are more readable to humans.

00:58:28.720 --> 00:58:30.970
It's an interesting trade-off.

00:58:30.970 --> 00:58:34.150
There have been lots of research
about whether CoTs are faithful

00:58:34.150 --> 00:58:35.720
and they're not truly faithful.

00:58:35.720 --> 00:58:36.700
We know that.

00:58:36.700 --> 00:58:40.540
But maybe it's better to have
a slightly more faithful CoT

00:58:40.540 --> 00:58:41.680
than to have the extra--

00:58:41.680 --> 00:58:45.610
I don't know, half percentage
point performance on AIME.

00:58:45.610 --> 00:58:46.110
Yes.

00:58:46.110 --> 00:58:48.400
[INAUDIBLE]
translated at the end.

00:58:48.400 --> 00:58:51.970
But if what you care about is
that someone can read it, like--

00:58:55.310 --> 00:58:58.510
I'm sure they can do
translations or other kinds

00:58:58.510 --> 00:59:01.220
of postprocessing to
make the CoTs better.

00:59:01.220 --> 00:59:04.420
And in some ways, you
might think of OpenAI

00:59:04.420 --> 00:59:07.270
and these other vendors'
efforts to summarize the CoTs

00:59:07.270 --> 00:59:10.120
as being very similar, because
the raw CoTs are probably

00:59:10.120 --> 00:59:13.800
much messier, and then they
probably rationalize it away.

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:16.110
That is one way, and I think
that is an effective way

00:59:16.110 --> 00:59:18.210
to get interpretability.

00:59:18.210 --> 00:59:20.740
But I do think if you're
interested in somehow--

00:59:20.740 --> 00:59:22.860
if you aesthetically
believe that the raw CoT is

00:59:22.860 --> 00:59:27.340
very important for monitoring
and closer interpretability,

00:59:27.340 --> 00:59:29.560
then I do think you do
want something like this.

00:59:32.682 --> 00:59:34.945
Cool.

00:59:34.945 --> 00:59:39.870
So now we're going to
move on to Kimi K1.5.

00:59:39.870 --> 00:59:41.640
And why do we study this one?

00:59:41.640 --> 00:59:45.240
If you look at the timestamps
for when R1 and K1.5 were

00:59:45.240 --> 00:59:48.540
released, it's contemporaneous.

00:59:48.540 --> 00:59:51.430
And it achieves very
similar results.

00:59:51.430 --> 00:59:54.010
It does so using
outcome-based rewards in RL.

00:59:54.010 --> 00:59:55.630
It doesn't use the
same algorithm.

00:59:55.630 --> 00:59:58.750
It has different details and
different interesting insights.

00:59:58.750 --> 01:00:01.680
And so we can learn about what's
the same, what's different,

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:06.450
and what parts are maybe
important in this process.

01:00:06.450 --> 01:00:08.690
So I'll just show you
the headline result

01:00:08.690 --> 01:00:11.300
before we start just
to get you to believe

01:00:11.300 --> 01:00:15.140
that this is a paper that's
worth being discussed here.

01:00:15.140 --> 01:00:18.480
Kimi K1.5 is the
dark blue bars here.

01:00:18.480 --> 01:00:22.320
You can see OpenAI's o1
as the next highest bar.

01:00:22.320 --> 01:00:25.940
So they're beating or
matching o1 across the board

01:00:25.940 --> 01:00:28.220
on a bunch of important tasks.

01:00:28.220 --> 01:00:32.760
And they do basically
things similar to R1.

01:00:32.760 --> 01:00:34.730
So they do SFT, they do RL.

01:00:34.730 --> 01:00:37.610
They have a different RL
algorithm, but they do RL.

01:00:37.610 --> 01:00:39.950
They also describe their
data set construction

01:00:39.950 --> 01:00:41.310
in a little bit more detail.

01:00:41.310 --> 01:00:43.770
And this actually gets
used in Qwen 3 later.

01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:46.550
So it's worth discussing.

01:00:46.550 --> 01:00:48.800
So let me talk through data.

01:00:48.800 --> 01:00:50.870
As Percy said
earlier, maybe data

01:00:50.870 --> 01:00:53.190
is the most important thing
in your whole pipeline.

01:00:53.190 --> 01:00:55.130
And so we should
always pay attention

01:00:55.130 --> 01:00:59.390
when people talk about their
kind of data curation strategies

01:00:59.390 --> 01:01:01.880
in a large-scale training paper.

01:01:01.880 --> 01:01:05.930
And so Kimi 1.5
does several things

01:01:05.930 --> 01:01:08.170
to try to curate their data set.

01:01:08.170 --> 01:01:11.250
So what they first do
is they try to balance

01:01:11.250 --> 01:01:12.730
across different domains.

01:01:12.730 --> 01:01:15.030
They have an automated,
I'm guessing,

01:01:15.030 --> 01:01:19.680
LLM-based tagging system
to categorize basically

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:22.390
math questions by different
domains and disciplines.

01:01:22.390 --> 01:01:24.000
And then they
balance across these

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:27.030
to try to get diversity
across different domains.

01:01:27.030 --> 01:01:28.860
They exclude, even
though these are

01:01:28.860 --> 01:01:31.445
verifiable multiple-choice
and true/false questions,

01:01:31.445 --> 01:01:32.820
because they argue
that these are

01:01:32.820 --> 01:01:35.770
too easy to hack or
to randomly guess.

01:01:35.770 --> 01:01:38.280
So they're only looking
for verifiable answers

01:01:38.280 --> 01:01:40.320
that are short and
can be evaluated

01:01:40.320 --> 01:01:44.670
by things like regex or LLM.

01:01:44.670 --> 01:01:46.770
And this is maybe the
most interesting piece

01:01:46.770 --> 01:01:48.390
of the curation here.

01:01:48.390 --> 01:01:51.720
What they do is they take
the model that doesn't do

01:01:51.720 --> 01:01:54.720
any reasoning, their SFT model.

01:01:54.720 --> 01:01:59.130
And they have this model
generate 10 answers,

01:01:59.130 --> 01:02:02.400
and the pass rate
is used in order

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:06.890
to determine whether to
include that example or not.

01:02:06.890 --> 01:02:11.780
And I think the exact thing they
use for their later selection

01:02:11.780 --> 01:02:13.970
strategy is they only
select examples that

01:02:13.970 --> 01:02:15.210
fail best of eight.

01:02:15.210 --> 01:02:19.050
So if they can get any
one out of eight correct,

01:02:19.050 --> 01:02:22.070
then it's excluded
for being too easy.

01:02:22.070 --> 01:02:25.272
SFT data similar to R1,
very little description.

01:02:25.272 --> 01:02:26.730
Who knows where
they got that from?

01:02:26.730 --> 01:02:28.740
They just say they do
some prompt engineering.

01:02:28.740 --> 01:02:30.840
So clearly, it was distilled
from something else,

01:02:30.840 --> 01:02:34.730
but we don't really know what
they distilled it off of.

01:02:34.730 --> 01:02:36.480
So I'll talk about
the RL algorithm.

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:38.340
The Kimi one is
kind of interesting.

01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:39.810
It's a different variation.

01:02:39.810 --> 01:02:44.030
It's actually maybe
closer to DPO in a way,

01:02:44.030 --> 01:02:47.210
but you end up with
algorithm that I think

01:02:47.210 --> 01:02:48.540
you'll very much recognize.

01:02:48.540 --> 01:02:51.020
So you can think of this
in a very interesting way

01:02:51.020 --> 01:02:54.270
as convergent evolution
of RL algorithms.

01:02:54.270 --> 01:02:56.490
So you start once
again at the very top.

01:02:56.490 --> 01:03:01.410
This is our classic goal where
it's sampling from a data set.

01:03:01.410 --> 01:03:03.030
We're sampling from our policy.

01:03:03.030 --> 01:03:04.670
We want to maximize rewards.

01:03:04.670 --> 01:03:07.880
We don't want to be too
far from our base policy.

01:03:07.880 --> 01:03:10.150
So this is the KL regularizer.

01:03:10.150 --> 01:03:12.980
If you remember
our DPO derivation,

01:03:12.980 --> 01:03:14.570
you make a nonparametric
assumption.

01:03:14.570 --> 01:03:18.110
You say pi star is the optimal
policy is an arbitrary function.

01:03:18.110 --> 01:03:20.080
That means that
the reward can be

01:03:20.080 --> 01:03:24.350
written as the log normalizer
plus the ratio of policies.

01:03:24.350 --> 01:03:27.040
This is the exact same
thing we did in DPO.

01:03:27.040 --> 01:03:30.670
And now, in DPO, what we did
was we took these rewards

01:03:30.670 --> 01:03:34.330
and we plugged them into
the Bradley-Terry preference

01:03:34.330 --> 01:03:35.210
function.

01:03:35.210 --> 01:03:36.548
We don't have this here.

01:03:36.548 --> 01:03:38.090
We're not doing
pairwise preferences,

01:03:38.090 --> 01:03:39.830
so we don't actually
take that step.

01:03:39.830 --> 01:03:41.470
Instead, we write
down this equation

01:03:41.470 --> 01:03:44.620
and we say we know that
for the optimal policy,

01:03:44.620 --> 01:03:46.678
we're going to
have equality here.

01:03:46.678 --> 01:03:48.220
And so all we're
going to do is we're

01:03:48.220 --> 01:03:50.380
going to make this a
difference, and we're going

01:03:50.380 --> 01:03:51.667
to add a squared loss on top.

01:03:51.667 --> 01:03:54.250
We're going to try to drive the
left and the right sides close

01:03:54.250 --> 01:03:56.280
by just adding a squared loss.

01:03:56.280 --> 01:03:57.530
It's a reasonable thing to do.

01:03:57.530 --> 01:03:59.470
People have done things
like this before.

01:03:59.470 --> 01:04:00.680
And this gives us our loss.

01:04:00.680 --> 01:04:04.350
It's basically trying to drive
the right side and the left side

01:04:04.350 --> 01:04:08.160
of something that should be an
equality for the optimal policy

01:04:08.160 --> 01:04:09.600
close together.

01:04:09.600 --> 01:04:12.810
And well, this is a little bit
of an exotic-looking object

01:04:12.810 --> 01:04:14.860
or maybe exotic-looking
initially.

01:04:14.860 --> 01:04:18.810
But if you take the gradient,
it just looks a lot like GRPO.

01:04:18.810 --> 01:04:21.480
You've got your gradients
of your policy--

01:04:21.480 --> 01:04:23.670
this is your policy
gradient stuff,

01:04:23.670 --> 01:04:25.272
and you've got a
baseline reward.

01:04:25.272 --> 01:04:26.980
And actually, what's
the baseline reward?

01:04:26.980 --> 01:04:31.110
I'm just going to average
the R within my batch.

01:04:31.110 --> 01:04:33.520
So here, this is actually
doing something different.

01:04:33.520 --> 01:04:37.530
This is the normalizing
constant I think over the batch.

01:04:37.530 --> 01:04:40.290
But we're doing
essentially similar kinds

01:04:40.290 --> 01:04:41.890
of baselining as GRPO.

01:04:41.890 --> 01:04:45.870
And we've got a slightly
different square of the log loss

01:04:45.870 --> 01:04:47.665
regularization to
keep my policy close

01:04:47.665 --> 01:04:48.790
rather than doing clipping.

01:04:48.790 --> 01:04:51.970
So zooming out,
what's happening here?

01:04:51.970 --> 01:04:54.390
Very similar to GRPO
in that this first part

01:04:54.390 --> 01:04:56.880
is a baseline loss, but
there's no standard deviation

01:04:56.880 --> 01:04:57.670
thing happening.

01:04:57.670 --> 01:05:00.070
The second part is analogous
to the clipping that

01:05:00.070 --> 01:05:02.330
happens in GRPO, but
instead of doing clipping,

01:05:02.330 --> 01:05:04.780
we're explicitly
regularizing the policy.

01:05:04.780 --> 01:05:06.460
So we've got the same
ingredients, just

01:05:06.460 --> 01:05:08.050
in a slightly different form.

01:05:08.050 --> 01:05:10.150
And so hopefully,
you can see that

01:05:10.150 --> 01:05:11.817
as long as you have
this policy gradient

01:05:11.817 --> 01:05:13.650
thing and the right
baselining and something

01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:15.160
that looks like
regularization, you

01:05:15.160 --> 01:05:18.400
can get a working RL algorithm.

01:05:18.400 --> 01:05:21.230
The other thing that the Kimi
folks do, and in some ways,

01:05:21.230 --> 01:05:23.800
I think this is
more forward-looking

01:05:23.800 --> 01:05:26.770
or they got this more right
than the R1 folks, which

01:05:26.770 --> 01:05:29.320
is that they realize that if
you're shipping a reasoning

01:05:29.320 --> 01:05:32.240
model, what you really care
about is the inference cost.

01:05:32.240 --> 01:05:33.920
And if you care
about inference cost,

01:05:33.920 --> 01:05:38.470
you had better try to control
the length of your CoTs.

01:05:38.470 --> 01:05:40.415
If you have really
long thinking chains,

01:05:40.415 --> 01:05:42.040
that's going to cost
you a ton of money

01:05:42.040 --> 01:05:44.240
or cost your users
a ton of money.

01:05:44.240 --> 01:05:47.060
And so instead of celebrating
the really long CoTs,

01:05:47.060 --> 01:05:50.980
the Kimi folks say, we want to
really compress the CoTs as much

01:05:50.980 --> 01:05:53.710
as possible while
keeping performance high.

01:05:53.710 --> 01:05:57.900
And so they have this
length reward thing here

01:05:57.900 --> 01:06:01.080
for what they're doing
is for each batch,

01:06:01.080 --> 01:06:03.760
they're looking at the maximum
and the minimum lengths.

01:06:03.760 --> 01:06:05.670
And what you have
is lambda, where

01:06:05.670 --> 01:06:09.990
lambda is roughly like, where
are you in the range of lengths

01:06:09.990 --> 01:06:10.980
within your batch?

01:06:10.980 --> 01:06:16.350
So if you're at plus 0.5, you're
really short, and negative 0.5,

01:06:16.350 --> 01:06:19.050
you are really long.

01:06:19.050 --> 01:06:21.270
And the reward is
basically going

01:06:21.270 --> 01:06:23.980
to be lambda whenever you
get the answer to be correct.

01:06:23.980 --> 01:06:25.560
So if your answer
is correct, you're

01:06:25.560 --> 01:06:28.140
going to incentivize yourself
to be really short at the very

01:06:28.140 --> 01:06:31.740
shortest end of this range,
whereas if your answers are

01:06:31.740 --> 01:06:34.170
incorrect, you're in this
bottom part of this length

01:06:34.170 --> 01:06:37.920
reward, which means that
you're incentivizing the CoT

01:06:37.920 --> 01:06:42.000
lengths to be roughly shorter
than the center of the range

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:42.790
of the rollouts.

01:06:42.790 --> 01:06:45.550
This is a somewhat funky
loss to me, to be honest,

01:06:45.550 --> 01:06:48.570
but you can understand the
dynamics of this loss, which

01:06:48.570 --> 01:06:52.290
is you're incentivizing
correct answers to be

01:06:52.290 --> 01:06:53.500
as short as possible.

01:06:53.500 --> 01:06:56.340
An incorrect answers are
incentivized to be average-ish,

01:06:56.340 --> 01:07:00.500
and so you don't have as strong
of an optimization pressure

01:07:00.500 --> 01:07:04.100
to push down incorrect
answers to be short.

01:07:04.100 --> 01:07:07.250
And one final note
on this length stuff

01:07:07.250 --> 01:07:10.490
is the Kimi folks
realize that if you

01:07:10.490 --> 01:07:13.320
add this reward
early on in training,

01:07:13.320 --> 01:07:16.820
it stalls RL because it
basically forces the model

01:07:16.820 --> 01:07:18.360
to say, I'm in a local minimum.

01:07:18.360 --> 01:07:19.950
I don't get any of
my answers correct.

01:07:19.950 --> 01:07:22.290
The best I can do is to
have my CoTs really short,

01:07:22.290 --> 01:07:24.720
and then you just can't get
out of that local minima.

01:07:24.720 --> 01:07:26.637
And so they actually
only turned this on later

01:07:26.637 --> 01:07:27.510
on during training.

01:07:27.510 --> 01:07:30.290
So they initially do a
bit of unconstrained RL

01:07:30.290 --> 01:07:35.540
and then they add this
length reward in afterwards.

01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:37.830
And they also have
additional code details.

01:07:37.830 --> 01:07:40.860
Who knows how much of this
stuff is necessary or important?

01:07:40.860 --> 01:07:43.080
But they actually have a
whole curriculum set up.

01:07:43.080 --> 01:07:45.420
They basically have
assigned difficulty labels.

01:07:45.420 --> 01:07:46.400
So the data set--

01:07:46.400 --> 01:07:50.480
just kind of top down, they
just manually-- or via LLMs,

01:07:50.480 --> 01:07:52.850
annotate the difficulty labels.

01:07:52.850 --> 01:07:55.140
And then they go from easy
to hard in that order.

01:07:55.140 --> 01:07:57.650
And then they also,
as they're going on,

01:07:57.650 --> 01:08:00.300
sample problems proportional
to 1 minus success rate.

01:08:00.300 --> 01:08:02.210
So if you're 100%
succeeding, you just

01:08:02.210 --> 01:08:05.930
never sample that
question ever again.

01:08:05.930 --> 01:08:08.690
And for the rewards,
they basically--

01:08:08.690 --> 01:08:11.120
for code, they take problems
with ground truth solutions

01:08:11.120 --> 01:08:13.190
and they generate a
bunch of test cases.

01:08:13.190 --> 01:08:16.010
And for math, they
basically use--

01:08:16.010 --> 01:08:19.520
actually, a reward model
where that's used to compare

01:08:19.520 --> 01:08:22.450
ground truth, human-written
answers to the LLM output.

01:08:22.450 --> 01:08:24.200
So instead of using
something like a regex

01:08:24.200 --> 01:08:27.359
or using SymPy, which is
what other people have done,

01:08:27.359 --> 01:08:28.910
the Kimi folks
actually use a model

01:08:28.910 --> 01:08:30.090
to do equivalence checking.

01:08:30.090 --> 01:08:32.960
It's kind of surprising to do
this in a verifiable reward

01:08:32.960 --> 01:08:35.880
case, but they don't
seem to have a problem.

01:08:35.880 --> 01:08:37.663
The reward models are
very, very accurate

01:08:37.663 --> 01:08:39.080
because it's really
all it's doing

01:08:39.080 --> 01:08:40.247
is advanced string matching.

01:08:42.859 --> 01:08:47.359
One of the things that's really
cool about the Kimi paper

01:08:47.359 --> 01:08:49.520
is that they also
talk about the infra

01:08:49.520 --> 01:08:52.720
issues that arise doing RL.

01:08:52.720 --> 01:08:56.430
I don't think I've seen any of
the other RL reasoning papers

01:08:56.430 --> 01:08:58.689
actually talk about
systems almost at all.

01:08:58.689 --> 01:09:00.810
So it's nice to see
that they're talking

01:09:00.810 --> 01:09:04.529
about this, what their structure
is, what their layout of this

01:09:04.529 --> 01:09:06.479
is.

01:09:06.479 --> 01:09:08.340
You'll have to deal
with this in A5,

01:09:08.340 --> 01:09:10.020
I think a very mini
version of this

01:09:10.020 --> 01:09:13.390
as you implement things
like rollouts in RL.

01:09:13.390 --> 01:09:15.960
But one thing I'll
note right is why is RL

01:09:15.960 --> 01:09:17.620
so hard to make efficient?

01:09:17.620 --> 01:09:20.310
In many ways, it's
harder than normal

01:09:20.310 --> 01:09:24.600
pretraining to have your GPUs
fully utilized during RL.

01:09:24.600 --> 01:09:26.609
And the reason I think
is because there's

01:09:26.609 --> 01:09:27.640
rollouts involved.

01:09:27.640 --> 01:09:30.071
So you have to be
generating sequences.

01:09:30.071 --> 01:09:31.779
And whenever you're
generating sequences,

01:09:31.779 --> 01:09:36.100
not only are slow in the
sense of inference is slow,

01:09:36.100 --> 01:09:38.290
but also you have
this other issue,

01:09:38.290 --> 01:09:43.120
which is that you have to switch
from RL to inference and back.

01:09:43.120 --> 01:09:45.970
And you have to be passing
of data back to the RL worker

01:09:45.970 --> 01:09:48.510
and the RL worker has
to pass model weights

01:09:48.510 --> 01:09:50.620
to the inference
server and vice versa.

01:09:50.620 --> 01:09:53.840
So you've got all this message
passing that can happen.

01:09:53.840 --> 01:09:57.530
And finally, this is
unique to long CoT models.

01:09:57.530 --> 01:09:59.200
But if you have
really long CoTs,

01:09:59.200 --> 01:10:01.340
batches can become
very, very uneven.

01:10:01.340 --> 01:10:05.770
So you have to handle
that cleverly somehow.

01:10:05.770 --> 01:10:10.510
And so the Kimi folks do this
relatively nice but fairly

01:10:10.510 --> 01:10:13.750
standard thing of having
different workers that

01:10:13.750 --> 01:10:15.650
are assigned to
do the RL updates,

01:10:15.650 --> 01:10:18.340
having different workers
assigned to do inference.

01:10:18.340 --> 01:10:20.530
And they have basically
message passing

01:10:20.530 --> 01:10:23.632
to be able to pass the weights
to the inference worker,

01:10:23.632 --> 01:10:25.840
and the inference worker
can basically make data sets

01:10:25.840 --> 01:10:28.300
for the RL worker.

01:10:28.300 --> 01:10:31.180
And they have almost
the same kinds of setups

01:10:31.180 --> 01:10:32.140
that you will have.

01:10:32.140 --> 01:10:34.150
They use vLLM for inference.

01:10:34.150 --> 01:10:37.120
And they also do
very advanced stuff

01:10:37.120 --> 01:10:39.770
where they have to have
vLLM with dummy weights,

01:10:39.770 --> 01:10:42.070
and they have to kill it
because of the complexities

01:10:42.070 --> 01:10:45.915
of doing this passing of weights
from one worker to another.

01:10:48.840 --> 01:10:50.880
Finally, I think
one thing that I

01:10:50.880 --> 01:10:54.150
think is interesting
that the Kimi people

01:10:54.150 --> 01:10:58.380
show in their results is they
have per-iteration results where

01:10:58.380 --> 01:11:02.740
they show as RL proceeds,
how does performance scale?

01:11:02.740 --> 01:11:05.260
And they show really nice
scaling, which is in the blue.

01:11:05.260 --> 01:11:08.380
And they also show the growth
of the length of the responses.

01:11:08.380 --> 01:11:10.770
And much like in
Dr. GRPO, because I

01:11:10.770 --> 01:11:14.650
don't think their RL
algorithm is inherently

01:11:14.650 --> 01:11:19.860
biased towards longer
responses, most of the response

01:11:19.860 --> 01:11:22.745
is actually plateau
out at a target length

01:11:22.745 --> 01:11:26.340
like we saw with Dr. GRPO,
rather than grow unboundedly

01:11:26.340 --> 01:11:30.180
like we did with
the vanilla GRPO.

01:11:30.180 --> 01:11:31.680
Cool.

01:11:31.680 --> 01:11:35.540
Any questions on Kimi before we
finally conclude with Qwen 3?

01:11:39.390 --> 01:11:45.940
So back to the RL setup
in detail inside of Kimi,

01:11:45.940 --> 01:11:48.260
I was wondering-- so
I guess when you're

01:11:48.260 --> 01:11:52.470
doing the rollouts during
training, the robots,

01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:54.650
the inference is
done by vLLM, right?

01:11:54.650 --> 01:11:55.530
Yes.

01:11:55.530 --> 01:11:57.410
And as I update
the parameter, that

01:11:57.410 --> 01:12:00.170
would mean that as
they update the model,

01:12:00.170 --> 01:12:06.170
it's also to sync that to
the vLLM inference worker.

01:12:06.170 --> 01:12:08.220
And that's what this
is for [INAUDIBLE].

01:12:08.220 --> 01:12:08.720
Yeah.

01:12:08.720 --> 01:12:11.010
So I think the
most annoying part,

01:12:11.010 --> 01:12:14.940
at least with current
libraries of this process,

01:12:14.940 --> 01:12:17.990
is essentially that step
of taking RL weights

01:12:17.990 --> 01:12:19.790
and putting it into vLLM.

01:12:19.790 --> 01:12:21.800
There is an
experimental API that

01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:27.455
is supposed to allow you to use
NCCL collective calls to shove

01:12:27.455 --> 01:12:30.398
a set of weights into vLLM.

01:12:30.398 --> 01:12:32.940
We were thinking about having
to use that for the assignment,

01:12:32.940 --> 01:12:33.600
actually.

01:12:33.600 --> 01:12:36.200
But it has too many
undocumented parameters for it

01:12:36.200 --> 01:12:37.850
to be a little bit mature.

01:12:37.850 --> 01:12:39.360
And so maybe next
year's iteration,

01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:41.790
this will actually be
fairly mature technology.

01:12:41.790 --> 01:12:43.490
But right now, I
think a lot of people

01:12:43.490 --> 01:12:46.400
do things start a vLLM
with dummy weights,

01:12:46.400 --> 01:12:49.060
the weights then get
loaded somehow into memory

01:12:49.060 --> 01:12:52.330
with hacks on top of the vLLM.

01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:55.780
And each iteration, they often
tear down vLLM to make sure

01:12:55.780 --> 01:12:59.200
they can free the
GPU memory fully.

01:12:59.200 --> 01:13:00.160
There's a lot of--

01:13:00.160 --> 01:13:04.370
I think the RL for LLMs I
think is fairly new still.

01:13:04.370 --> 01:13:07.030
So I think the infrastructural
support remains a little bit

01:13:07.030 --> 01:13:09.730
immature, but I think in maybe
a year things will actually just

01:13:09.730 --> 01:13:10.940
get much nicer.

01:13:10.940 --> 01:13:12.670
Yeah, question back there.

01:13:12.670 --> 01:13:17.360
You have a lot of accuracy
rewards and other rewards.

01:13:17.360 --> 01:13:21.442
How are you supposed to
combine them together?

01:13:21.442 --> 01:13:24.640
So how do you combine
the different rewards?

01:13:24.640 --> 01:13:26.590
I think this is one of the RL--

01:13:26.590 --> 01:13:30.130
not quite black magic, but
really RL magic things.

01:13:30.130 --> 01:13:31.370
You just tune weights.

01:13:31.370 --> 01:13:33.670
In all cases, all
the rewards are just

01:13:33.670 --> 01:13:35.443
added together,
but with weights,

01:13:35.443 --> 01:13:37.360
and how are the weights
determined empirically

01:13:37.360 --> 01:13:40.720
in order to maximize the
downstream performance.

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:42.470
Especially for things
like format rewards,

01:13:42.470 --> 01:13:45.910
you can almost think of them as
shaping or surrogate rewards.

01:13:45.910 --> 01:13:49.000
Don't necessarily really care
about the formatting rewards.

01:13:49.000 --> 01:13:51.780
It's more of a means to an
end to get a good long CoT

01:13:51.780 --> 01:13:52.660
within the tags.

01:13:52.660 --> 01:13:53.868
That will get you the answer.

01:13:56.530 --> 01:13:57.030
Cool.

01:13:57.030 --> 01:13:57.730
OK.

01:13:57.730 --> 01:14:00.820
So the final one I want
to talk about is Qwen 3.

01:14:00.820 --> 01:14:03.283
And thankfully, Qwen 3
released their report

01:14:03.283 --> 01:14:04.450
before the end of the class.

01:14:04.450 --> 01:14:06.390
I get to include it.

01:14:06.390 --> 01:14:09.430
And I think this is the
most recent and modern

01:14:09.430 --> 01:14:14.650
of the RL for reasoning
models to have come out.

01:14:14.650 --> 01:14:17.040
And so we can see
how they've built

01:14:17.040 --> 01:14:20.250
upon the previous works,
where they've changed things,

01:14:20.250 --> 01:14:22.320
and they have actually
pretty interesting scaling

01:14:22.320 --> 01:14:26.220
and data results that
are new and unique.

01:14:26.220 --> 01:14:29.460
So the overall picture
is very similar to what

01:14:29.460 --> 01:14:31.230
R1 and Kimi have done.

01:14:31.230 --> 01:14:34.410
So Qwen basically take
their base models.

01:14:34.410 --> 01:14:36.820
They'll do a long CoT SFT stage.

01:14:36.820 --> 01:14:38.410
So that's this first
stage over here.

01:14:38.410 --> 01:14:40.020
They'll do their reasoning RL.

01:14:40.020 --> 01:14:42.520
They'll do something funky that
I'll talk about later called

01:14:42.520 --> 01:14:43.820
thinking mode fusion.

01:14:43.820 --> 01:14:46.798
And then they'll do
RLHF RL, and then that's

01:14:46.798 --> 01:14:47.840
the model that they ship.

01:14:47.840 --> 01:14:49.923
Of course, they then distill
that in various ways,

01:14:49.923 --> 01:14:52.250
but we can forget about
that for the moment.

01:14:52.250 --> 01:14:54.800
So we've already
seen this in R1.

01:14:54.800 --> 01:14:57.220
RLHF comes after
reasoning and then

01:14:57.220 --> 01:15:00.580
distillation comes
yet after that.

01:15:00.580 --> 01:15:02.960
And we already know a
lot of the playbooks.

01:15:02.960 --> 01:15:05.110
So I can actually go
through this fairly fast.

01:15:05.110 --> 01:15:07.720
Much like Kimi, they
basically curate the data

01:15:07.720 --> 01:15:10.150
by filtering for
difficulty using best-of-n.

01:15:10.150 --> 01:15:12.220
So if your base model
that hasn't been RL

01:15:12.220 --> 01:15:15.260
can already answer it,
if you sample n times,

01:15:15.260 --> 01:15:18.220
then you can just get rid of it.

01:15:18.220 --> 01:15:20.385
They also do some
decontamination things

01:15:20.385 --> 01:15:21.760
where they remove
things that are

01:15:21.760 --> 01:15:23.950
too similar to validation data.

01:15:23.950 --> 01:15:26.980
And then they also
manually filter

01:15:26.980 --> 01:15:30.820
essentially their SFT set, so
for their initial SFT data.

01:15:30.820 --> 01:15:32.800
For long CoT, they
manually filter it

01:15:32.800 --> 01:15:35.050
for whether they are guessing
or whether they actually

01:15:35.050 --> 01:15:35.650
got it right.

01:15:35.650 --> 01:15:38.500
The one thing that empirically
I think is really interesting

01:15:38.500 --> 01:15:42.270
about the Qwen 3 RL results is
that they are actually doing

01:15:42.270 --> 01:15:48.810
this RL only on 3,995 examples,
which is a very small number

01:15:48.810 --> 01:15:51.090
of examples to be doing this on.

01:15:51.090 --> 01:15:53.830
And they get pretty good
gains out of the RL process.

01:15:53.830 --> 01:15:58.740
And so you can view this
as RL on verified rewards

01:15:58.740 --> 01:15:59.980
as being very efficient.

01:15:59.980 --> 01:16:03.810
You could also think of this as
being analogous to many sample

01:16:03.810 --> 01:16:05.260
efficiency results in the past.

01:16:05.260 --> 01:16:07.980
People have shown that
you can instruction

01:16:07.980 --> 01:16:09.520
tune a model with
very few samples,

01:16:09.520 --> 01:16:12.962
or that you can distill long
CoT with very few samples.

01:16:12.962 --> 01:16:14.670
But that doesn't
necessarily mean that it

01:16:14.670 --> 01:16:16.230
doesn't continue to scale.

01:16:16.230 --> 01:16:17.800
We don't really why.

01:16:17.800 --> 01:16:20.650
What this does show is that
even with very few examples,

01:16:20.650 --> 01:16:25.140
you can sometimes do RL,
which is surprising and cool.

01:16:25.140 --> 01:16:29.760
So what is the two
new Qwen-specific

01:16:29.760 --> 01:16:31.620
stuff that they do?

01:16:31.620 --> 01:16:33.810
The thing that they do
is this thing called

01:16:33.810 --> 01:16:37.530
thinking mode fusion, which
I think is interesting, where

01:16:37.530 --> 01:16:40.340
I think the field or the
various trends are going

01:16:40.340 --> 01:16:42.470
is in controlling inference.

01:16:42.470 --> 01:16:45.590
So what they do is they
want to have both a thinking

01:16:45.590 --> 01:16:49.070
model and a nonthinking
model in the same single set

01:16:49.070 --> 01:16:49.668
of parameters.

01:16:49.668 --> 01:16:50.460
So what do they do?

01:16:50.460 --> 01:16:53.870
Well, after they
trained a model with RL,

01:16:53.870 --> 01:16:55.710
they have a model
that can do thinking.

01:16:55.710 --> 01:16:57.770
And now they're going
to fine-tune it again

01:16:57.770 --> 01:16:58.887
to do one of two things.

01:16:58.887 --> 01:17:01.220
They're going to fine-tune
the model with some data that

01:17:01.220 --> 01:17:02.960
has a think tag,
and then it's going

01:17:02.960 --> 01:17:04.132
to do the normal CoT thing.

01:17:04.132 --> 01:17:05.840
And you can get this
data from yourself--

01:17:05.840 --> 01:17:08.720
the original thinking
model can generate this--

01:17:08.720 --> 01:17:11.660
or you can have a no_think
tag, in which case, it should

01:17:11.660 --> 01:17:14.270
immediately emit an answer.

01:17:14.270 --> 01:17:17.570
And in this case, they're
going to have to supervise,

01:17:17.570 --> 01:17:19.620
fine-tune the model to
know what no_think means,

01:17:19.620 --> 01:17:23.780
and to immediately try
to emit the answer.

01:17:23.780 --> 01:17:27.860
And one interesting
side effect of this

01:17:27.860 --> 01:17:30.560
is that they found that if they
do this training, where they

01:17:30.560 --> 01:17:33.480
train the model to have a
think and a no_think tag,

01:17:33.480 --> 01:17:35.970
then what they can
do is they can--

01:17:35.970 --> 01:17:37.690
if the model continues
to think and you

01:17:37.690 --> 01:17:39.530
want to terminate
the thinking process,

01:17:39.530 --> 01:17:42.400
you can actually terminate
the thinking process

01:17:42.400 --> 01:17:44.110
at a special string.

01:17:44.110 --> 01:17:45.860
Considering the limited
time of the user,

01:17:45.860 --> 01:17:48.280
I have to give a solution
by thinking directly now,

01:17:48.280 --> 01:17:51.590
and then n think tag, and then
it accurately gives the answer.

01:17:51.590 --> 01:17:53.140
So this gives them
a control knob

01:17:53.140 --> 01:17:57.580
by which they can more precisely
control a maximum number

01:17:57.580 --> 01:17:59.680
of thinking tokens.

01:17:59.680 --> 01:18:03.550
And this gives them a pretty
clean test time scaling

01:18:03.550 --> 01:18:05.060
from a single model.

01:18:05.060 --> 01:18:08.050
And so they can set a
maximum thinking budget.

01:18:08.050 --> 01:18:11.410
And of course, the very,
very maximum out to infinity

01:18:11.410 --> 01:18:13.220
is just the original
thinking model.

01:18:13.220 --> 01:18:15.520
But they can early
terminate to the left

01:18:15.520 --> 01:18:17.950
and they can get
graceful degradations

01:18:17.950 --> 01:18:19.250
in model performance.

01:18:19.250 --> 01:18:21.560
That's not too bad at
the very beginning.

01:18:21.560 --> 01:18:24.010
You can have the thinking
tokens and still get

01:18:24.010 --> 01:18:28.210
some pretty good performance.

01:18:28.210 --> 01:18:30.610
And so you can look at--

01:18:30.610 --> 01:18:33.490
Qwen 3 also does a nice
ablation where they give you

01:18:33.490 --> 01:18:36.240
the performance at the
reasoning RL stage,

01:18:36.240 --> 01:18:38.100
at the thinking
mode fusion stage,

01:18:38.100 --> 01:18:41.270
and at the general RL stage.

01:18:41.270 --> 01:18:43.190
And one thing that's
very interesting to me

01:18:43.190 --> 01:18:46.440
here is that if you look at
the first two sets of rows,

01:18:46.440 --> 01:18:49.100
the general task and the
instruction following ones,

01:18:49.100 --> 01:18:52.080
reasoning RL helps,
thinking mode fusion helps,

01:18:52.080 --> 01:18:54.390
and of course, RLHF continues
to help here as well.

01:18:54.390 --> 01:18:57.810
It kind of everything
helps in this regime.

01:18:57.810 --> 01:19:01.415
But if you look at math
or STEM performance,

01:19:01.415 --> 01:19:04.820
in the thinking case,
general RL hurts performance

01:19:04.820 --> 01:19:07.500
and in the nonthinking
case, it helps performance.

01:19:07.500 --> 01:19:11.450
So there really seems to be at
least somewhat of a trade off

01:19:11.450 --> 01:19:15.110
in, do I optimize for
general-purpose instruction

01:19:15.110 --> 01:19:18.920
following up here, or do I
optimize for math and coding

01:19:18.920 --> 01:19:20.170
down here?

01:19:20.170 --> 01:19:23.490
And so those are interesting
properties that are emerging.

01:19:23.490 --> 01:19:27.740
And it'll be cool to see
more future models sidestep

01:19:27.740 --> 01:19:31.840
these trade offs somehow.

01:19:31.840 --> 01:19:33.440
OK, cool.

01:19:33.440 --> 01:19:38.410
So to put that all together,
our initial motivation

01:19:38.410 --> 01:19:40.700
was to say RL is very powerful.

01:19:40.700 --> 01:19:43.930
We figured out that we can
do RL with language models

01:19:43.930 --> 01:19:46.600
in the RLHF domain,
but you can't just hill

01:19:46.600 --> 01:19:50.260
climb on noisy pairwise
preferences forever.

01:19:50.260 --> 01:19:52.780
So one solution
is to pick domains

01:19:52.780 --> 01:19:55.970
in which you can't do reward
hacking and then just go for it.

01:19:55.970 --> 01:19:59.020
RL in narrow domains
is one good solution.

01:19:59.020 --> 01:20:02.000
And GRPO is one very
simple algorithm.

01:20:02.000 --> 01:20:04.160
And hopefully, you all
have a sense of OK,

01:20:04.160 --> 01:20:06.910
I can just do policy gradients
with some good baselines

01:20:06.910 --> 01:20:10.060
and that will enable RL
on all of these kinds

01:20:10.060 --> 01:20:12.250
of verifiable reward domains.

01:20:12.250 --> 01:20:14.920
And then finally, there's
lots of successful recipes

01:20:14.920 --> 01:20:16.510
in the wild, and
hopefully now you've

01:20:16.510 --> 01:20:18.620
seen what's in common,
what's different,

01:20:18.620 --> 01:20:21.580
what implementation
tricks matter.

01:20:21.580 --> 01:20:22.930
Cool.

01:20:22.930 --> 01:20:27.420
Thanks a lot, and I will
see you all next week.
